{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DvLMx5xvhje5uBWrnZJirF","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# MASH TV\n2\t\n3\t*A playful riff on Smash TV. I brainstormed fifty riffs on the source's name and numbered them, then drew one with Python's\n4\t`secrets.randbelow(50) + 1`. It came up 9: **MASH TV**. Don't re-pick the title.*\n5\t\n6\t**Pitch.** MASH TV is a 1–4 player twin-stick arena shooter set inside the deadliest game show on television. Contestants\n7\tfight through single-screen studio rooms while goons, robots and worse pour in through four doors. They grab cash and\n8\tridiculous prizes, play to the cameras to keep the **ratings** up, and pick a route through the studio map. The goal is to\n9\tsurvive three episodes, each capped by a giant boss, and reach the Season Finale. Collect enough Golden Keys and the\n10\tsecret **Green Room** opens at the end. Your host is the immortal, oily, sequinned **Lance Lustre**: *\"KA-CHING! KA-BOOM!\"*\n11\t\n12\t**Names and files** (use the model id and timestamp from *your* TASK.md):\n13\t\n14\t| What | Value |\n15\t|---|---|\n16\t| Title on screen | `MASH TV` (sub-line: `LIVE FROM STUDIO 9`) |\n17\t| GameTitle in file names | `MashTV` |\n18\t| SOURCE in file names | `SmashTV` |\n19\t| Dev folder (all dev files) | `MashTV-SmashTV-<model>-<timestamp>/` |\n20\t| Deliverable (root, beside TASK.md) | `MashTV-SmashTV-<model>-<timestamp>.html` |\n21\t| Exit interview (root, beside the HTML) | `exit-interview.json` |\n22\t| \"Plan\" field in Source Information | `PLAN.md` |\n23\t\n24\t---\n25\t\n26\t## 0. Quick facts\n27\t\n28\t| | |\n29\t|---|---|\n30\t| Genre | Twin-stick arena shooter (arcade). Co-op for 1–4 players, with a route choice between rooms. |\n31\t| Perspective | One fixed single-screen arena per room, seen from a high three-quarter \"studio camera\": the engine view `threequarter` (pitch 55). `topdown` (pitch 80) is a Settings option. |\n32\t| Engine | my-3D2dge handoff package **v0.85.2** (`github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge`, `dist/handoff/`; commit `4390a98` when planned). Use the full edition, `my-3d2dge.js`, which includes every module (we use `arcade`). |\n33\t| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client `aihub-session 1.4.0`, **mode `shared-world`**, 1–4 players. |\n34\t| Local multiplayer | 1–4 players on one screen: P1 on keyboard+mouse or a pad, P2–P4 on pads (drop-in with Options/Start). |\n35\t| Length | A full run takes about 20–25 minutes: three episodes of 2–4 rooms plus a boss, then the bonus Green Room. A hub session round takes about 5–6 minutes. |\n36\t| Output | One self-contained HTML file with the engine, the hub client and the game inlined. No image or audio files; everything is drawn and synthesized in code. |\n37\t\n38\t---\n39\t\n40\t## 1. Ground rules for the builder\n41\t\n42\t1. **TASK.md is the contract; this plan is how to meet it.** Where they disagree, TASK.md wins.\n43\t2. **Work only inside your dev folder.** Other agents share the parent directories, ports and processes. Read nothing\n44\t   outside your folder except what you fetch:\n45\t   - Engine: shallow-clone it **inside your dev folder** (e.g. `.cache/my-3d2dge`; when this plan was written, `/tmp` on this\n46\t     machine was a full tmpfs) with `git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge .cache/my-3d2dge`.\n47\t     Copy `dist/handoff/*` into the dev folder: the engine, the docs, the tools, the kits and `src/assets`.\n48\t   - Hub: **don't clone ai-hub** (it's over 1 GB). Fetch the four files you need:\n49\t     `curl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/{session.js,harness.html,bridge.js,round.js}`\n50\t     into `vendor/hub/`. `lib/hub/README.md` is the protocol; read it once, whole.\n51\t3. **Servers and processes.** Prefer `file://` and headless Node. The hub harness needs a server: bind `127.0.0.1` on port 0\n52\t   (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints the port, or a Node `http` server on port 0). Before trusting a\n53\t   result, check that the page is your game (`window.MT` exists and the title is `MASH TV`). Stop only processes you\n54\t   started, by PID. Never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`.\n55\t4. **Read the engine docs before writing code.** Read `API.md` whole. Read `docs/ASSETS.md` whole before drawing props.\n56\t   Grep `API-INDEX.md` for names; read the `AI_GUIDE.md` sections on Standard features, Frame order, Players, Testing\n57\t   and the Twin-stick recipe. Every API name in this plan was checked against v0.85.2. If your copy is newer, grep the index.\n58\t5. **Engine patches are allowed but kept small**, each listed with its reason in `engine/PATCHES.md`. That list feeds the\n59\t   Engine Feedback. The patches this plan needs are in §15.5.\n60\t6. **No TODOs, placeholders, stubs or \"coming soon\" anywhere.** Every menu item works; every feature in this plan ships.\n61\t7. **One test command** (`npm test` → `node tools/test.mjs`) prints only failures and a one-line count. Rerun only the\n62\t   suites whose code you changed (`--only <suite>`), and run everything before you hand off. Use screenshots only for\n63\t   what a script can't check (§17.3). As TASK.md says, don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or\n64\t   audit.\n65\t\n66\t---\n67\t\n68\t## 2. What makes Smash TV distinctive, and how MASH TV makes it play\n69\t\n70\t| Source trait | MASH TV mechanic or feature |\n71\t|---|---|\n72\t| A lethal TV game show played as entertainment, with a smarmy host | **Lance Lustre** reacts to everything on a **jumbotron** above the arena, with a lower-third subtitle and gibberish voice. The **Ratings** system turns \"be entertaining\" into a mechanic: it drives a cash multiplier, Prime Time, and producer \"stunts\" when you dawdle. |\n73\t| Hordes flooding in through four doors | Door-spawn wave director: each door opens, enemies pour through and the door slams shut. Up to ~60 enemies on screen. |\n74\t| Two-stick 8-way shooting while moving | Analog twin-stick (pad), mouse aim (PC), and 8-way arrow-key fire (keyboard only). |\n75\t| Power-ups, cash and absurd consumer prizes | Timed weapons, cash × the ratings multiplier, and named joke prizes (\"A SELF-AWARE TOASTER\"). Co-host **Penny Prize** tallies them in a Prize Recap after each episode. |\n76\t| A room-to-room map with path choice | Episode maps (3×3 grid). After a clear, the exits light up with the destination's name; walk through one to choose. Longer paths hold keys and cash; shorter paths carry less risk. |\n77\t| Giant, multi-part bosses | **Mashy** the mascot (shoot off its parts), the **Wheel of Misfortune** (each spin picks the attack), **Lance in the Starmaker 9000**, then **The Network** itself. |\n78\t| A secret bonus for collectors | **Golden Keys** (9 in the game). With 5 or more after the finale, you reach the **Green Room** and the true ending. |\n79\t| Simultaneous co-op | 1–4 players in one arena, locally or online (AI Hub shared world). Ratings and keys are shared; cash is individual. |\n80\t\n81\t**Tone:** loud, garish and cartoon-violent. Enemies burst into **confetti, sparks and smoke** (no blood: \"BY ORDER OF THE\n82\tFCC, ALL GORE HAS BEEN REPLACED WITH CONFETTI!\"). It's consumerist satire with a retro-future TV aesthetic: neon, chrome,\n83\tmarquee bulbs, sponsor logos. **Writing style:** short, shouted, ALL-CAPS announcer lines with punchlines and fake sponsor\n84\tplugs, like the source's barker energy. Every line is original (§8).\n85\t\n86\t---\n87\t\n88\t## 3. The game at a glance\n89\t\n90\t**Core loop (one sentence):** enter a sealed studio room, survive the waves pouring from its doors while grabbing cash,\n91\tprizes and power-ups, then pick the next door on the studio map, until you reach and beat the episode's boss.\n92\t\n93\t**Flow:**\n94\t\n95\t```\n96\tBoot ─► Title ─(10 s idle)─► Attract demo ─(any input)─► Title\n97\tTitle menu: START THE SHOW · EPISODES · HOW TO PLAY · TOP EARNERS · OPTIONS · ABOUT & CREDITS\n98\tSTART THE SHOW ─► Difficulty ─► Contestant Lobby (join 1–4) ─► Cold Open ─► Episode 1 card\n99\t  ─► Room ─► (clear) ─► choose exit ─► \"NEXT ON MASH TV\" map card ─► Room … ─► Boss ─► Prize Recap\n100\t  ─► Episode 2 card … Boss ─► Prize Recap ─► Episode 3 card … Finale (Lance ─► The Network) ─► Prize Recap\n101\t  ─► keys ≥ 5 ? Green Room ─► Ending B (true ending) : Ending A\n102\t  ─► Initials (each qualifying player) ─► Top Earners ─► Title\n103\tAll lives lost (every player out) ─► CONTINUE? 10…0 (✕/Enter: continue, −50 % winnings) ─► else Game Over ─► Initials ─► Top Earners ─► Title\n104\tAI Hub session: Our own card (contestant line-up) ─► 3-2-1 ─► Lightning Round (3 rooms + Mashy) ─► finish ─► end card\n105\t```\n106\t\n107\t---\n108\t\n109\t## 4. Controls and input\n110\t\n111\t### 4.1 Bindings\n112\t\n113\t| Action | DualSense (default glyphs) | Xbox glyphs (Settings) | Keyboard + mouse |\n114\t|---|---|---|---|\n115\t| Move | L-stick, D-pad | L-stick, D-pad | W A S D |\n116\t| Aim + fire | R-stick (fires while pushed past 0.35) | R-stick | Mouse aims (reticle); **hold Left click** fires |\n117\t| Fire (in the last aim direction) | R2 (hold) | RT | — |\n118\t| 8-way fire (keyboard only) | — | — | Arrow keys (hold; two arrows = diagonal) |\n119\t| Slide (dodge with i-frames) | L2, or ✕ | LT, or A | Space or Shift |\n120\t| Showstopper (screen bomb) | R1, or ○ | RB, or B | E or Right click |\n121\t| Showboat (taunt for ratings) | L1, or △ | LB, or Y | Q |\n122\t| Pause / menu | Options | Menu | Esc or P |\n123\t| Menus: move / confirm / back | D-pad, L-stick / ✕ / ○ | D-pad / A / B | Arrows, WASD / Enter, Space / Esc, Backspace; mouse hover focuses, click picks, wheel scrolls |\n124\t\n125\tThe face buttons are alternates for players who don't use the right stick. While twin-sticking, the thumbs are on the\n126\tsticks, so every combat action is on a shoulder or trigger. All actions can be rebound on the engine's standard Controls\n127\tscreen.\n128\t\n129\t### 4.2 Engine input presets\n130\t\n131\tDefine two presets and switch between them. Arrows must **aim** in play but **navigate** in menus. The engine keeps\n132\tarrows on the moves only if a preset lists them, so the play preset leaves them out.\n133\t\n134\t```js\n135\t// A preset lists every action it uses, the menu's own (start, pause, confirm, cancel) included.\n136\t// Mouse clicks in menus go through input.clicked() on the hovered item, not through 'confirm'.\n137\tconst MASH_MENU = { up: ['KeyW','ArrowUp','Pad12'], down: ['KeyS','ArrowDown','Pad13'], left: ['KeyA','ArrowLeft','Pad14'],\n138\t  right: ['KeyD','ArrowRight','Pad15'], start: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Pad9'], pause: ['Escape','KeyP','Pad9'],\n139\t  confirm: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Space','Pad0'], cancel: ['Escape','Backspace','Pad1'],\n140\t  tabL: ['KeyQ','Pad4'], tabR: ['KeyE','Pad5'], reset: ['KeyR','Pad3'] };          // tabs/reset: dev mode & pages\n141\tconst MASH_PLAY = { up: ['KeyW','Pad12'], down: ['KeyS','Pad13'], left: ['KeyA','Pad14'], right: ['KeyD','Pad15'],\n142\t  aimUp: ['ArrowUp'], aimDown: ['ArrowDown'], aimLeft: ['ArrowLeft'], aimRight: ['ArrowRight'],\n143\t  fire: ['Mouse0','Pad7'], slide: ['Space','ShiftLeft','ShiftRight','Pad6','Pad0'],\n144\t  bomb: ['KeyE','Mouse2','Pad5','Pad1'], showboat: ['KeyQ','Pad4','Pad3'],\n145\t  start: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Pad9'], pause: ['Escape','KeyP','Pad9'],\n146\t  confirm: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Pad0'], cancel: ['Escape','Backspace','Pad1'] };\n147\t```\n148\t\n149\t- Set `input: MASH_MENU` on front-end scenes and `input: MASH_PLAY` on play scenes (the scene's `input` field).\n150\t- **Preset sync hook (engine patch P1, §15.5).** While the pause menu (the Shell) or one of our overlays is open in play,\n151\t  every player's input must use `MASH_MENU`, so arrows move the focus. Expose the engine's internal `HOOKS` as `E.HOOKS`\n152\t  and push an input hook. The hook runs after `input.tick` and before the Shell reads navigation. It calls\n153\t  `p.use(want)` on each of `game.players` when the wanted preset changes: `MENU` if `game.paused || MT.overlay.open`,\n154\t  else the scene's preset. Changing presets calls `clear()`, which is fine at those moments.\n155\t- Joined players (`game.addPlayer`) copy P1's preset when they join. The hook keeps them in sync afterwards.\n156\t- Name the actions on the Controls screen with `menu.controls`: `{ fire: 'Fire', slide: 'Slide', bomb: 'Showstopper',\n157\t  showboat: 'Showboat', aimUp: 'Fire up (keyboard)', … , tabL: false, tabR: false, reset: false }`.\n158\t\n159\t### 4.3 How a contestant reads its intent each step\n160\t\n161\t`intentFrom(input, contestant)` returns `{ move:[x,y], aim: angle|null, fire: bool, slide: bool(pressed), bomb:\n162\tbool(pressed), showboat: bool(pressed) }`:\n163\t\n164\t1. **Move:** `input.move()` is in screen directions. Convert it with `game.view.screenDirToGround(mx, my)`.\n165\t2. **Aim**, in priority order:\n166\t   - The right stick, `input.padAim` (non-null past the dead zone; length > 0.35). Aim = the stick's screen direction\n167\t     converted to ground. Fire is true if Auto-fire is on (the default).\n168\t   - Arrow keys held: an 8-way direction from `aimUp/Down/Left/Right`. Fire is true.\n169\t   - The mouse, when `input.lastDevice === 'mouse'` or the mouse moved within the last 2 s. Aim = the angle from the\n170\t     contestant to `game.mouseGround()`. Fire = `down('fire')` (Left click).\n171\t   - Otherwise keep the last aim. `down('fire')` (R2) fires along it; if there's no aim yet, use the facing.\n172\t3. **Aim assist** (Settings, on by default for pads only): if an enemy is within 170 units and within ±10° of the aim,\n173\t   rotate the aim halfway toward it.\n174\t4. **Facing** follows the aim while firing; otherwise it follows movement.\n175\t\n176\tBots (attract mode, tests, the dev \"bot partner\") and remote players (§14) produce the same intent object. A contestant\n177\tnever reads an `E.Input` directly.\n178\t\n179\t### 4.4 Prompts and glyphs\n180\t\n181\t- Prompts follow `input.lastDevice`. On `gamepad`, show the button glyph in the family from the setting `glyphs`. On\n182\t  `keyboard`/`mouse`, show the keycap or mouse icon for that device's binding. Find the code with\n183\t  `input.map[action]` (first `Pad…` for pads; first key or `Mouse…` for keyboard and mouse); `input.codeLabel(code)`\n184\t  gives the text.\n185\t- **The 5×7 font has no ✕ ○ □ △.** Build `src/02-glyphs.js`: small pixel icons, 11×11 at scale 1, drawn with `E.px`.\n186\t  - PlayStation: ✕ blue `#7aa8ff`, ○ red `#ff6a6a`, □ pink `#ff8ad8` and △ green `#5fe0a0`, each on a dark disc. L1, R1,\n187\t    L2 and R2 are rounded tabs. Options and Create are pills; the sticks are L/R ringed discs; plus a D-pad cross.\n188\t  - Xbox: A green, B red, X blue and Y yellow letter discs; LB, RB, LT, RT, Menu and View.\n189\t  - Keyboard: a keycap with the label (1–6 chars; wide caps for Space, Shift, Enter and Esc).\n190\t  - Mouse: a mouse body with the left or right half lit.\n191\t\n192\t  API: `MT.glyph.draw(g, x, y, action, input, { scale })` returns the width it drew;\n193\t  `MT.glyph.prompt(g, x, y, [[action, 'TEXT'], …], input)` draws a row of icons with labels.\n194\t- **Engine patch P2:** the standard `glyphs` setting defaults to `'playstation'`, its options are\n195\t  `[['playstation','PlayStation'],['xbox','Xbox']]`, and its `apply` sets `glyphs` on **every** `game.players` input. A\n196\t  player who joins later inherits it.\n197\t\n198\t---\n199\t\n200\t## 5. Systems and mechanics\n201\t\n202\tAll numbers below are defaults. Every one marked **(T)** is a live tuning value with a dev slider (§13). Logic always reads\n203\t`MT.T.<key>` and never a literal.\n204\t\n205\t### 5.1 Contestants (the players)\n206\t\n207\t- **Body:** `new E.Body({ x, y, r: 6 })`. Speed `playerSpeed` **(T)** 95 u/s (×1.45 with Speed Sneakers), analog,\n208\t  colliding with the arena's walls, closed doors and solid props.\n209\t- **Rig:** `new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.1, build: 'heroic', weapon: 'gun', … })`, dressed per look (§9.3). Each step:\n210\t  `rig.update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, point: aiming, hurt: hurtT > 0, flip: slideU, pose, expr })`. **Game\n211\t  logic never reads rig joints:** the muzzle is `x + cos(aim) * 10, y + sin(aim) * 10, z 14`.\n212\t- **HP and lives by difficulty:**\n213\t  - ROOKIE: HP 5, lives 5, enemy speed ×0.85, enemy bullets ×0.8.\n214\t  - CONTESTANT (default): HP 3, lives 3.\n215\t  - CHAMPION: HP 1, lives 3, enemy speed ×1.15. This is the classic one-hit rule.\n216\t\n217\t  Extra life at $250,000, $750,000, $1,500,000 and $3,000,000 per contestant, with an \"ENCORE!\" banner and the `oneup` sfx.\n218\t- **Getting hit** (no i-frames active): −1 HP (−2 from a brute charge or a boss slam).\n219\t  - 1.2 s invulnerability (`invulnTime` **(T)**), blinking at 12 Hz.\n220\t  - Knockback 120, `game.hitFx` (small), `input.rumble(.5, .15)`, ratings −12, a \"wrong answer\" buzzer and the crowd's \"OOOH\".\n221\t- **Death (HP 0):**\n222\t  - A \"MASHED!\" pop-up, a confetti burst in the player's colour, `pose: 'die'` then `down: 1`, and a Lance quip.\n223\t  - −1 life. The weapon and add-ons reset.\n224\t  - 2.0 s later the contestant **drops in on a cable** from the ceiling, under a spotlight near the entry door: z 70 → 0\n225\t    over 0.5 s. It has 2.5 s invulnerability and at least 1 Showstopper.\n226\t- **Out of lives (co-op):** state `out`, HUD \"OUT — PRESS START TO CONTINUE\".\n227\t  - Pressing Start (Options/Enter) brings the contestant back with full lives and **half their winnings** (the producer's\n228\t    cut). The run keeps going for the others.\n229\t  - When **every** contestant is out at once, the global CONTINUE countdown runs (§5.10).\n230\t- **Slide** (`slide`, pressed): 0.28 s at `slideSpeed` **(T)** 235 u/s in the move direction (or the facing if standing).\n231\t  - I-frames for the first 0.22 s. It can fire while sliding.\n232\t  - Cooldown `slideCooldown` **(T)** 0.85 s. Dust puff and a whoosh.\n233\t  - The rig rolls: pass `flip: u` (0→1 over the slide).\n234\t- **Showstopper** (`bomb`, pressed, stock > 0; max 3; 2 at the start of a run):\n235\t  - A 0.12 s white `game.flash` (it follows the comfort settings), a camera-flashbulb burst sound and a boom.\n236\t  - Ring particles and `particles.glints` everywhere.\n237\t  - Every enemy on screen takes 25 damage; boss parts take 30. All foe bullets are cleared, and enemies are stunned for 1.0 s.\n238\t  - Ratings +8. A 0.08 s `game.freeze` (not in sessions).\n239\t- **Showboat** (`showboat`, pressed, 3 s cooldown): a 0.6 s pose. Cycle `pose: 'cheer' | 'hips' | 'wave'` with\n240\t  `expr: 'smile'`; no moving or firing.\n241\t  - When it completes, let n = enemies within 64 units and m = enemies within 32. Ratings gain\n242\t    `4 + 3n + 3m` (cap 30), and a pop-up \"STYLE +N\".\n243\t  - Crowd cheer, louder with the gain. A hit during the pose cancels the gain.\n244\t  - This is how the show's theme becomes a risk/reward mechanic.\n245\t- **Walk-in:** at room `ENTER`, contestants walk from the entry door to a 2×2 formation 40 units inside. They are\n246\t  invulnerable and ignore input for 1.2 s.\n247\t- **Local drop-in:** `game.allowJoin({ max: 4, keys: false, onJoin })` stays open on every play scene. A joining pad gets\n248\t  the next free slot, colour and look, $0, default lives, and drops in on a cable.\n249\t\n250\t### 5.2 Weapons and power-ups\n251\t\n252\t| id | On-screen name | Kind | Behaviour | Duration (T: `puDuration` ×) |\n253\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n254\t| `blaster` | STUDIO BLASTER | primary (default) | 9 shots/s (`fireRate` T), bolt dmg 1 (`dmgMul` T), speed 330 (`bulletSpeed` T), r 2.5, life 0.9 s | ∞ |\n255\t| `spread` | TRIPLE PLAY | primary | 8/s, 3 bolts at −13°/0°/+13°, dmg 1 each | 14 s |\n256\t| `rockets` | PRIME-TIME ROCKETS | primary | 3.5/s, a rocket accelerating 180→300, direct dmg 4 + splash 3 (r 24), smoke trail, explodes on a hit, a wall or end of life (0.9 s) | 12 s |\n257\t| `popcorn` | POPCORN POPPER | primary | 2.5/s, a lobbed bag (`E.arc`) landing 90 ahead (or at the reticle if closer), splash 2 (r 18) + 6 kernels outward (dmg 1.5, speed 200, life 0.35, pierce 1). Clears crowds behind cover. | 12 s |\n258\t| `laser` | LIMELIGHT LASER | primary | a continuous beam from the muzzle to the first wall, piercing every enemy on the line, 16 DPS (`16*dt` per step), turn rate limited to 7 rad/s, a hum loop | 10 s |\n259\t| `records` | GOLD RECORDS | add-on | 2 gold discs orbit at r 26, 4.2 rad/s; each touch does 2 dmg (0.15 s cooldown per enemy) and destroys small foe bullets (orb, shard, coin, card, static) | 15 s |\n260\t| `shield` | SPONSOR SHIELD | add-on | invulnerable bubble with a flickering \"KRUNCH-O\" logo; touching enemies take 1 dmg per 0.2 s | 8 s |\n261\t| `speed` | SPEED SNEAKERS | add-on | move ×1.45, slide cooldown ×0.6, afterimages (`r.actor(..., { ghost })`) | 10 s |\n262\t| `bomb` | SHOWSTOPPER | instant | +1 stock (at max 3: +$2,000 instead) | — |\n263\t| `life` | ENCORE | instant | +1 life | — |\n264\t\n265\t- One **primary** slot. A new primary replaces the old one; the same primary refreshes its timer. Add-ons stack with the\n266\t  primary and with each other.\n267\t- In the HUD, the weapon icon has a draining bar. The last 2 s blink with a tick. At expiry a small \"BACK TO BASICS\".\n268\t- **Projectile tech:**\n269\t  - Blaster and spread bolts, the kernels and every foe bullet: `E.Bullets` pools on the `'ground'` plane, `pShots`\n270\t    (team `p`, `data: { owner, w }`) and `fShots` (team `f`, `data: { id, kind }`; ids are needed for netplay).\n271\t  - Rockets and popcorn bags are small custom projectile lists (update, draw and explode).\n272\t  - The laser is a per-step segment test against enemy circles; its length comes from stepping the ray against\n273\t    `map.heightAt` (or `map.ray`).\n274\t- Player bullets stop at walls and closed doors (`shots.update(dt, map)`).\n275\t\n276\t### 5.3 Pickups and economy\n277\t\n278\t| Pickup | Look (string sprite, ~10 px, bobbing, glow) | Effect |\n279\t|---|---|---|\n280\t| Bill | green banknote | $100 |\n281\t| Stack | banded stack | $500 |\n282\t| Gold bar | gold ingot with glint | $1,000 |\n283\t| Briefcase | silver case with \"$\" | $5,000 |\n284\t| Prize box | gift box (red, blue or gold, with a bow) | a named prize from the episode's table (§7.6), worth its value |\n285\t| Golden Key | gold key, star glint | team key +1 (out of 9) |\n286\t| Capsule | a mini CRT TV whose screen shows the power-up icon and its letter (S spread, R rockets, P popcorn, L laser, G gold records, B shield bubble, F fast sneakers) | the power-up |\n287\t| Showstopper | a flashbulb | +1 bomb |\n288\t| Encore | a gold star with \"1UP\" | +1 life |\n289\t| Golden Statuette | a big gold \"LANCIE\" award statue | Green Room grand prize (ends the bonus) |\n290\t\n291\t- **Collection:** a contestant touches it (radius = contestant r + 7).\n292\t  - Cash and prizes are credited × the ratings multiplier × `cashMul` **(T)**, to the collector only.\n293\t  - A pop-up \"+$1,500\" in the collector's colour, plus \"ka-ching\".\n294\t- **Lifetime:** 9 s on the floor, blinking for the last 2.5 s. Keys, placed items and the statuette never expire.\n295\t- **Magnet:** once a room is clear, cash within 60 units drifts to the nearest contestant.\n296\t- **Prize box:**\n297\t  - Draws the next prize from the episode table, shuffled with the run's seed, no repeats until it runs out.\n298\t  - A banner: \"YOU WON: A SELF-AWARE TOASTER! ($2,500)\", with Penny's squeal (babble).\n299\t  - The prize joins that player's recap list.\n300\t- **Keys** are shared by the team. A banner \"GOLDEN KEY 4/9\" and a chime; Lance's key line the first time.\n301\t- **Drops** on enemy death roll the enemy's drop table (§5.8) × `dropMul` **(T)**. Floor caps: at most 3 capsules and\n302\t  25 cash pickups at once.\n303\t- **Prize crates** are placed props: HP 3, solid while whole (`map.solid`, or a static body). When broken they spill\n304\t  3–5 cash, a 50 % chance of a prize, and the key if the room's data says so.\n305\t\n306\t### 5.4 Ratings (the show's heartbeat)\n307\t\n308\t- `R` runs 0–100 and is **shared by the team**: it's the show's rating, not a player's. It's drawn top-centre as a big TV\n309\t  \"RATINGS\" gauge with a needle, the multiplier and an APPLAUSE light.\n310\t- **Gains:**\n311\t  - A kill adds the enemy's `rat` value. A multi-kill adds +2 for each kill past 2 within 0.4 s.\n312\t  - Showboat (§5.1).\n313\t  - A **near miss**: +0.5 when a foe bullet passes within 7 units of a contestant without hitting (at most 3 a second).\n314\t  - A prize +2, a key +5, a boss part destroyed +10.\n315\t  - All gains × `ratingsGain` **(T)**.\n316\t- **Losses:** a hit −12; a death −25; BANKRUPT (the wheel) −10.\n317\t- **Decay:** after 2.5 s without a gain, R falls by `ratingsDecay` **(T)** 2.5 a second.\n318\t- **Multiplier:** R < 20 ×1; < 40 ×2; < 60 ×3; < 80 ×4; ≥ 80 ×5.\n319\t- **PRIME TIME** when R reaches 100. It lasts `primeTime` **(T)** 12 s:\n320\t  - The multiplier is locked at ×5.\n321\t  - A \"PRIME TIME!\" banner and fanfare sting; `audio.layer('prime', 1, .3)` brings the brass in.\n322\t  - The audience throws 3 random capsules in arcs from the bleachers; confetti cannons fire; the APPLAUSE sign blinks.\n323\t  - Afterwards R = 60, with a 20 s cooldown before the next Prime Time.\n324\t- **RATINGS STUNT** (anti-stall): if R < 5 for 10 s during waves, \"WE'RE LOSING VIEWERS!\" and the producers release the\n325\t  episode's stunt from a random door:\n326\t  - Ep1: 12 clapbots.\n327\t  - Ep2: a dolly tank.\n328\t  - Ep3: a mascot brute.\n329\t\n330\t  R +15. At most once per room.\n331\t\n332\t### 5.5 Arena, doors and hazards\n333\t\n334\t**Geometry, the same in every room.** A TileMap of 23 × 15 cells is 368 × 240 world units.\n335\t- **Walls:** row 0, row 14, col 0 and col 22, wall type 1: `h: 26`, studio-flat faces (§9.2).\n336\t  - The south wall row is `cut: true`, so it never hides the floor from the camera.\n337\t- **Interior:** 21 × 13 cells, x 16–352, y 16–224, centre **(184, 120)**.\n338\t- **Door gaps** (3 cells, wall type 2, painted by our door painter):\n339\t  - N: cols 10–12 of row 0. S: cols 10–12 of row 14. W: rows 6–8 of col 0. E: rows 6–8 of col 22.\n340\t  - Door centres: N (184, 8), S (184, 232), W (8, 120), E (360, 120).\n341\t- **Room data uses interior cells** `[cx, cy]`, cx 0–20 and cy 0–12. In world units that's\n342\t  `(24 + cx * 16, 24 + cy * 16)`.\n343\t\n344\t**Door states:**\n345\t- `closed`.\n346\t- `opening`: 0.35 s; the shutter rolls up, a red beacon and a klaxon.\n347\t- `open`: spawning.\n348\t- `closing`: 0.3 s; a slam and dust.\n349\t- `exit`: a green beacon and a lit sign above the door with the destination's name and room-type icon. Its cells become\n350\t  walkable (`map.set(cx, cy, 0)`), with an exit trigger 10 units past the threshold.\n351\t\n352\t**Spawning:**\n353\t- From a **door:** enemies appear 20 units outside the gap and walk in with wall collision off (\"entering\") until inside\n354\t  the interior. The door stays open while its queue has spawns, plus 0.6 s.\n355\t- From the **audience** (hecklers): a leap from the E or W bleachers over the wall. An arc from x −30 or 398 to a random\n356\t  interior point on that side; 0.7 s, invulnerable in the air.\n357\t- **Drop:** from the ceiling at a random free interior point at least 50 units from every contestant. A shadow\n358\t  telegraphs for 0.6 s, then it falls.\n359\t\n360\t**Hazards:**\n361\t- **Conveyor belts** (Ep2): strips 2 cells tall across the room. Each pushes everything on it (contestants, enemies,\n362\t  pickups, the key) at ±40 u/s, with animated chevrons. Direction reverses every 12 s after a 1.5 s warning (lights, an\n363\t  alarm, the belt slowing).\n364\t- **Pyro vents** (Ep3): floor grates firing in data-driven patterns.\n365\t  - Telegraph: 1.0 s of glow, sparks and rumble.\n366\t  - Then a 1.2 s flame column, r 12: 1 damage to contestants, 3 damage a second to enemies. It's emissive and casts light.\n367\t- **Tomato splats:** puddles of r 14 for 6 s that slow contestants to ×0.6.\n368\t- **Prize crates** and **vault locks:** static targets (rooms 1-6, 2-6, 3-4).\n369\t\n370\t**Camera:**\n371\t- Fixed: `game.focus(184, 120, 0)` every update.\n372\t- Zoom is fitted so the walled arena fills the screen, leaving ~22 px top and bottom for the HUD. Measure\n373\t  `map.bounds(game.view)` at zoom 1 and recompute when the screen changes size (`game.screen.listeners`). Clamp to\n374\t  [0.6, 1.6], then × `camZoom` **(T)**.\n375\t- The scene's `camera: { zoom: false }` turns the player's zoom off. Shake goes through `game.shake` (comfort settings\n376\t  apply).\n377\t- Wide or tall screens show the studio around the arena (bleachers, cranes, the jumbotron; §9.2).\n378\t\n379\t### 5.6 Room flow (phases)\n380\t\n381\t`ENTER` → `INTRO` → `WAVES` → `CLEAR` → `EXIT` → `TRANSITION` → the next room's `ENTER`.\n382\t\n383\t- **ENTER** (1.2 s): the walk-in. The door behind the contestants slams.\n384\t- **INTRO** (≤ 2 s): Lance's room line on the jumbotron and a \"MASH!\" stinger. An episode's first room is preceded by the\n385\t  episode card (§11.3).\n386\t- **WAVES:** the room script (§5.7) runs.\n387\t- **CLEAR:** every wave has spawned and no counted enemy is alive. Mimes, crates and vault locks don't count.\n388\t  - \"ROOM CLEAR!\" banner and sting.\n389\t  - Each living contestant gets a clear bonus of `$1,000 × episode × multiplier`, +50 % if cleared under the room's\n390\t    `par` seconds.\n391\t  - A Lance line. Remaining foe bullets fade out.\n392\t- **EXIT:** the exit doors open with their signs. The first contestant into an exit trigger picks it; 0.8 s later every\n393\t  contestant goes. Pickups can still be collected meanwhile.\n394\t  - If nobody leaves within 25 s: \"HURRY UP!\" and 4 clapbots every 6 s from the doors that aren't exits.\n395\t- **TRANSITION:** a 0.4 s TV-static wipe, then the \"NEXT ON MASH TV\" map card (2.5 s, skippable with confirm), then\n396\t  another static wipe into the next room.\n397\t\n398\t**Boss rooms:** `ENTER` → `BOSS_INTRO` (~4 s cutscene, skippable) → `BOSS` → `BOSS_DEATH` (3 s) → `CLEAR` → the `recap`\n399\tscene.\n400\t\n401\t**Survive rooms** (1-4): `WAVES` ends when the timer runs out and the field is clear.\n402\t\n403\t### 5.7 Waves: the room script data format\n404\t\n405\t```js\n406\t// src/20-content-*.js — one room\n407\t{ id: '1-2', name: 'MIME FIELD', ep: 1, type: 'battle', cell: [0, 2], exits: { N: '1-4' }, par: 60,\n408\t  place: [['mime', 4, 3], ['mime', 10, 2] /* … */],      // [kind, cx, cy] at room start (interior cells)\n409\t  items: [[1.5, 'spread', [5, 6]]],                      // [seconds after WAVES starts, pickup, 'center' | 'drop' | [cx, cy]]\n410\t  key: { rule: 'mimes' },                                 // 'mimes' | 'place' | 'crate' | 'carrier' | 'vault' | 'final' | 'survive'\n411\t  hazards: [],                                            // e.g. { kind: 'conveyor', rows: [3, 6, 9], dirs: [1, -1, 1] }\n412\t  waves: [\n413\t    { at: 2, spawn: [['S', 'goon', 8]] },\n414\t    { left: 3, at: 8, spawn: [['N', 'popper', 2], ['E', 'goon', 8]] },\n415\t    { left: 3, at: 10, spawn: [['W', 'goon', 10]], items: [['rockets', 'drop']] },\n416\t  ] }\n417\t```\n418\t\n419\t- **When a wave starts:** `at` seconds after the previous wave started, or when no more than `left` counted enemies are\n420\t  alive, whichever comes first. With only one of them given, that one alone. Build it as one `game.script` per room:"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6976d6c-e5f5-464d-af16-4195491a03f7","uuid":"0f67079e-ffe3-4ece-847a-dcd5222f3323","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:03:11.949Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050259__x4339a3/work/PLAN.md","content":"# MASH TV\n\n*A playful riff on Smash TV. I brainstormed fifty riffs on the source's name and numbered them, then drew one with Python's\n`secrets.randbelow(50) + 1`. It came up 9: **MASH TV**. Don't re-pick the title.*\n\n**Pitch.** MASH TV is a 1–4 player twin-stick arena shooter set inside the deadliest game show on television. Contestants\nfight through single-screen studio rooms while goons, robots and worse pour in through four doors. They grab cash and\nridiculous prizes, play to the cameras to keep the **ratings** up, and pick a route through the studio map. The goal is to\nsurvive three episodes, each capped by a giant boss, and reach the Season Finale. Collect enough Golden Keys and the\nsecret **Green Room** opens at the end. Your host is the immortal, oily, sequinned **Lance Lustre**: *\"KA-CHING! KA-BOOM!\"*\n\n**Names and files** (use the model id and timestamp from *your* TASK.md):\n\n| What | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Title on screen | `MASH TV` (sub-line: `LIVE FROM STUDIO 9`) |\n| GameTitle in file names | `MashTV` |\n| SOURCE in file names | `SmashTV` |\n| Dev folder (all dev files) | `MashTV-SmashTV-<model>-<timestamp>/` |\n| Deliverable (root, beside TASK.md) | `MashTV-SmashTV-<model>-<timestamp>.html` |\n| Exit interview (root, beside the HTML) | `exit-interview.json` |\n| \"Plan\" field in Source Information | `PLAN.md` |\n\n---\n\n## 0. Quick facts\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Genre | Twin-stick arena shooter (arcade). Co-op for 1–4 players, with a route choice between rooms. |\n| Perspective | One fixed single-screen arena per room, seen from a high three-quarter \"studio camera\": the engine view `threequarter` (pitch 55). `topdown` (pitch 80) is a Settings option. |\n| Engine | my-3D2dge handoff package **v0.85.2** (`github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge`, `dist/handoff/`; commit `4390a98` when planned). Use the full edition, `my-3d2dge.js`, which includes every module (we use `arcade`). |\n| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client `aihub-session 1.4.0`, **mode `shared-world`**, 1–4 players. |\n| Local multiplayer | 1–4 players on one screen: P1 on keyboard+mouse or a pad, P2–P4 on pads (drop-in with Options/Start). |\n| Length | A full run takes about 20–25 minutes: three episodes of 2–4 rooms plus a boss, then the bonus Green Room. A hub session round takes about 5–6 minutes. |\n| Output | One self-contained HTML file with the engine, the hub client and the game inlined. No image or audio files; everything is drawn and synthesized in code. |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Ground rules for the builder\n\n1. **TASK.md is the contract; this plan is how to meet it.** Where they disagree, TASK.md wins.\n2. **Work only inside your dev folder.** Other agents share the parent directories, ports and processes. Read nothing\n   outside your folder except what you fetch:\n   - Engine: shallow-clone it **inside your dev folder** (e.g. `.cache/my-3d2dge`; when this plan was written, `/tmp` on this\n     machine was a full tmpfs) with `git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge .cache/my-3d2dge`.\n     Copy `dist/handoff/*` into the dev folder: the engine, the docs, the tools, the kits and `src/assets`.\n   - Hub: **don't clone ai-hub** (it's over 1 GB). Fetch the four files you need:\n     `curl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/{session.js,harness.html,bridge.js,round.js}`\n     into `vendor/hub/`. `lib/hub/README.md` is the protocol; read it once, whole.\n3. **Servers and processes.** Prefer `file://` and headless Node. The hub harness needs a server: bind `127.0.0.1` on port 0\n   (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints the port, or a Node `http` server on port 0). Before trusting a\n   result, check that the page is your game (`window.MT` exists and the title is `MASH TV`). Stop only processes you\n   started, by PID. Never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`.\n4. **Read the engine docs before writing code.** Read `API.md` whole. Read `docs/ASSETS.md` whole before drawing props.\n   Grep `API-INDEX.md` for names; read the `AI_GUIDE.md` sections on Standard features, Frame order, Players, Testing\n   and the Twin-stick recipe. Every API name in this plan was checked against v0.85.2. If your copy is newer, grep the index.\n5. **Engine patches are allowed but kept small**, each listed with its reason in `engine/PATCHES.md`. That list feeds the\n   Engine Feedback. The patches this plan needs are in §15.5.\n6. **No TODOs, placeholders, stubs or \"coming soon\" anywhere.** Every menu item works; every feature in this plan ships.\n7. **One test command** (`npm test` → `node tools/test.mjs`) prints only failures and a one-line count. Rerun only the\n   suites whose code you changed (`--only <suite>`), and run everything before you hand off. Use screenshots only for\n   what a script can't check (§17.3). As TASK.md says, don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or\n   audit.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What makes Smash TV distinctive, and how MASH TV makes it play\n\n| Source trait | MASH TV mechanic or feature |\n|---|---|\n| A lethal TV game show played as entertainment, with a smarmy host | **Lance Lustre** reacts to everything on a **jumbotron** above the arena, with a lower-third subtitle and gibberish voice. The **Ratings** system turns \"be entertaining\" into a mechanic: it drives a cash multiplier, Prime Time, and producer \"stunts\" when you dawdle. |\n| Hordes flooding in through four doors | Door-spawn wave director: each door opens, enemies pour through and the door slams shut. Up to ~60 enemies on screen. |\n| Two-stick 8-way shooting while moving | Analog twin-stick (pad), mouse aim (PC), and 8-way arrow-key fire (keyboard only). |\n| Power-ups, cash and absurd consumer prizes | Timed weapons, cash × the ratings multiplier, and named joke prizes (\"A SELF-AWARE TOASTER\"). Co-host **Penny Prize** tallies them in a Prize Recap after each episode. |\n| A room-to-room map with path choice | Episode maps (3×3 grid). After a clear, the exits light up with the destination's name; walk through one to choose. Longer paths hold keys and cash; shorter paths carry less risk. |\n| Giant, multi-part bosses | **Mashy** the mascot (shoot off its parts), the **Wheel of Misfortune** (each spin picks the attack), **Lance in the Starmaker 9000**, then **The Network** itself. |\n| A secret bonus for collectors | **Golden Keys** (9 in the game). With 5 or more after the finale, you reach the **Green Room** and the true ending. |\n| Simultaneous co-op | 1–4 players in one arena, locally or online (AI Hub shared world). Ratings and keys are shared; cash is individual. |\n\n**Tone:** loud, garish and cartoon-violent. Enemies burst into **confetti, sparks and smoke** (no blood: \"BY ORDER OF THE\nFCC, ALL GORE HAS BEEN REPLACED WITH CONFETTI!\"). It's consumerist satire with a retro-future TV aesthetic: neon, chrome,\nmarquee bulbs, sponsor logos. **Writing style:** short, shouted, ALL-CAPS announcer lines with punchlines and fake sponsor\nplugs, like the source's barker energy. Every line is original (§8).\n\n---\n\n## 3. The game at a glance\n\n**Core loop (one sentence):** enter a sealed studio room, survive the waves pouring from its doors while grabbing cash,\nprizes and power-ups, then pick the next door on the studio map, until you reach and beat the episode's boss.\n\n**Flow:**\n\n```\nBoot ─► Title ─(10 s idle)─► Attract demo ─(any input)─► Title\nTitle menu: START THE SHOW · EPISODES · HOW TO PLAY · TOP EARNERS · OPTIONS · ABOUT & CREDITS\nSTART THE SHOW ─► Difficulty ─► Contestant Lobby (join 1–4) ─► Cold Open ─► Episode 1 card\n  ─► Room ─► (clear) ─► choose exit ─► \"NEXT ON MASH TV\" map card ─► Room … ─► Boss ─► Prize Recap\n  ─► Episode 2 card … Boss ─► Prize Recap ─► Episode 3 card … Finale (Lance ─► The Network) ─► Prize Recap\n  ─► keys ≥ 5 ? Green Room ─► Ending B (true ending) : Ending A\n  ─► Initials (each qualifying player) ─► Top Earners ─► Title\nAll lives lost (every player out) ─► CONTINUE? 10…0 (✕/Enter: continue, −50 % winnings) ─► else Game Over ─► Initials ─► Top Earners ─► Title\nAI Hub session: Our own card (contestant line-up) ─► 3-2-1 ─► Lightning Round (3 rooms + Mashy) ─► finish ─► end card\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Controls and input\n\n### 4.1 Bindings\n\n| Action | DualSense (default glyphs) | Xbox glyphs (Settings) | Keyboard + mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Move | L-stick, D-pad | L-stick, D-pad | W A S D |\n| Aim + fire | R-stick (fires while pushed past 0.35) | R-stick | Mouse aims (reticle); **hold Left click** fires |\n| Fire (in the last aim direction) | R2 (hold) | RT | — |\n| 8-way fire (keyboard only) | — | — | Arrow keys (hold; two arrows = diagonal) |\n| Slide (dodge with i-frames) | L2, or ✕ | LT, or A | Space or Shift |\n| Showstopper (screen bomb) | R1, or ○ | RB, or B | E or Right click |\n| Showboat (taunt for ratings) | L1, or △ | LB, or Y | Q |\n| Pause / menu | Options | Menu | Esc or P |\n| Menus: move / confirm / back | D-pad, L-stick / ✕ / ○ | D-pad / A / B | Arrows, WASD / Enter, Space / Esc, Backspace; mouse hover focuses, click picks, wheel scrolls |\n\nThe face buttons are alternates for players who don't use the right stick. While twin-sticking, the thumbs are on the\nsticks, so every combat action is on a shoulder or trigger. All actions can be rebound on the engine's standard Controls\nscreen.\n\n### 4.2 Engine input presets\n\nDefine two presets and switch between them. Arrows must **aim** in play but **navigate** in menus. The engine keeps\narrows on the moves only if a preset lists them, so the play preset leaves them out.\n\n```js\n// A preset lists every action it uses, the menu's own (start, pause, confirm, cancel) included.\n// Mouse clicks in menus go through input.clicked() on the hovered item, not through 'confirm'.\nconst MASH_MENU = { up: ['KeyW','ArrowUp','Pad12'], down: ['KeyS','ArrowDown','Pad13'], left: ['KeyA','ArrowLeft','Pad14'],\n  right: ['KeyD','ArrowRight','Pad15'], start: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Pad9'], pause: ['Escape','KeyP','Pad9'],\n  confirm: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Space','Pad0'], cancel: ['Escape','Backspace','Pad1'],\n  tabL: ['KeyQ','Pad4'], tabR: ['KeyE','Pad5'], reset: ['KeyR','Pad3'] };          // tabs/reset: dev mode & pages\nconst MASH_PLAY = { up: ['KeyW','Pad12'], down: ['KeyS','Pad13'], left: ['KeyA','Pad14'], right: ['KeyD','Pad15'],\n  aimUp: ['ArrowUp'], aimDown: ['ArrowDown'], aimLeft: ['ArrowLeft'], aimRight: ['ArrowRight'],\n  fire: ['Mouse0','Pad7'], slide: ['Space','ShiftLeft','ShiftRight','Pad6','Pad0'],\n  bomb: ['KeyE','Mouse2','Pad5','Pad1'], showboat: ['KeyQ','Pad4','Pad3'],\n  start: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Pad9'], pause: ['Escape','KeyP','Pad9'],\n  confirm: ['Enter','NumpadEnter','Pad0'], cancel: ['Escape','Backspace','Pad1'] };\n```\n\n- Set `input: MASH_MENU` on front-end scenes and `input: MASH_PLAY` on play scenes (the scene's `input` field).\n- **Preset sync hook (engine patch P1, §15.5).** While the pause menu (the Shell) or one of our overlays is open in play,\n  every player's input must use `MASH_MENU`, so arrows move the focus. Expose the engine's internal `HOOKS` as `E.HOOKS`\n  and push an input hook. The hook runs after `input.tick` and before the Shell reads navigation. It calls\n  `p.use(want)` on each of `game.players` when the wanted preset changes: `MENU` if `game.paused || MT.overlay.open`,\n  else the scene's preset. Changing presets calls `clear()`, which is fine at those moments.\n- Joined players (`game.addPlayer`) copy P1's preset when they join. The hook keeps them in sync afterwards.\n- Name the actions on the Controls screen with `menu.controls`: `{ fire: 'Fire', slide: 'Slide', bomb: 'Showstopper',\n  showboat: 'Showboat', aimUp: 'Fire up (keyboard)', … , tabL: false, tabR: false, reset: false }`.\n\n### 4.3 How a contestant reads its intent each step\n\n`intentFrom(input, contestant)` returns `{ move:[x,y], aim: angle|null, fire: bool, slide: bool(pressed), bomb:\nbool(pressed), showboat: bool(pressed) }`:\n\n1. **Move:** `input.move()` is in screen directions. Convert it with `game.view.screenDirToGround(mx, my)`.\n2. **Aim**, in priority order:\n   - The right stick, `input.padAim` (non-null past the dead zone; length > 0.35). Aim = the stick's screen direction\n     converted to ground. Fire is true if Auto-fire is on (the default).\n   - Arrow keys held: an 8-way direction from `aimUp/Down/Left/Right`. Fire is true.\n   - The mouse, when `input.lastDevice === 'mouse'` or the mouse moved within the last 2 s. Aim = the angle from the\n     contestant to `game.mouseGround()`. Fire = `down('fire')` (Left click).\n   - Otherwise keep the last aim. `down('fire')` (R2) fires along it; if there's no aim yet, use the facing.\n3. **Aim assist** (Settings, on by default for pads only): if an enemy is within 170 units and within ±10° of the aim,\n   rotate the aim halfway toward it.\n4. **Facing** follows the aim while firing; otherwise it follows movement.\n\nBots (attract mode, tests, the dev \"bot partner\") and remote players (§14) produce the same intent object. A contestant\nnever reads an `E.Input` directly.\n\n### 4.4 Prompts and glyphs\n\n- Prompts follow `input.lastDevice`. On `gamepad`, show the button glyph in the family from the setting `glyphs`. On\n  `keyboard`/`mouse`, show the keycap or mouse icon for that device's binding. Find the code with\n  `input.map[action]` (first `Pad…` for pads; first key or `Mouse…` for keyboard and mouse); `input.codeLabel(code)`\n  gives the text.\n- **The 5×7 font has no ✕ ○ □ △.** Build `src/02-glyphs.js`: small pixel icons, 11×11 at scale 1, drawn with `E.px`.\n  - PlayStation: ✕ blue `#7aa8ff`, ○ red `#ff6a6a`, □ pink `#ff8ad8` and △ green `#5fe0a0`, each on a dark disc. L1, R1,\n    L2 and R2 are rounded tabs. Options and Create are pills; the sticks are L/R ringed discs; plus a D-pad cross.\n  - Xbox: A green, B red, X blue and Y yellow letter discs; LB, RB, LT, RT, Menu and View.\n  - Keyboard: a keycap with the label (1–6 chars; wide caps for Space, Shift, Enter and Esc).\n  - Mouse: a mouse body with the left or right half lit.\n\n  API: `MT.glyph.draw(g, x, y, action, input, { scale })` returns the width it drew;\n  `MT.glyph.prompt(g, x, y, [[action, 'TEXT'], …], input)` draws a row of icons with labels.\n- **Engine patch P2:** the standard `glyphs` setting defaults to `'playstation'`, its options are\n  `[['playstation','PlayStation'],['xbox','Xbox']]`, and its `apply` sets `glyphs` on **every** `game.players` input. A\n  player who joins later inherits it.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Systems and mechanics\n\nAll numbers below are defaults. Every one marked **(T)** is a live tuning value with a dev slider (§13). Logic always reads\n`MT.T.<key>` and never a literal.\n\n### 5.1 Contestants (the players)\n\n- **Body:** `new E.Body({ x, y, r: 6 })`. Speed `playerSpeed` **(T)** 95 u/s (×1.45 with Speed Sneakers), analog,\n  colliding with the arena's walls, closed doors and solid props.\n- **Rig:** `new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.1, build: 'heroic', weapon: 'gun', … })`, dressed per look (§9.3). Each step:\n  `rig.update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, point: aiming, hurt: hurtT > 0, flip: slideU, pose, expr })`. **Game\n  logic never reads rig joints:** the muzzle is `x + cos(aim) * 10, y + sin(aim) * 10, z 14`.\n- **HP and lives by difficulty:**\n  - ROOKIE: HP 5, lives 5, enemy speed ×0.85, enemy bullets ×0.8.\n  - CONTESTANT (default): HP 3, lives 3.\n  - CHAMPION: HP 1, lives 3, enemy speed ×1.15. This is the classic one-hit rule.\n\n  Extra life at $250,000, $750,000, $1,500,000 and $3,000,000 per contestant, with an \"ENCORE!\" banner and the `oneup` sfx.\n- **Getting hit** (no i-frames active): −1 HP (−2 from a brute charge or a boss slam).\n  - 1.2 s invulnerability (`invulnTime` **(T)**), blinking at 12 Hz.\n  - Knockback 120, `game.hitFx` (small), `input.rumble(.5, .15)`, ratings −12, a \"wrong answer\" buzzer and the crowd's \"OOOH\".\n- **Death (HP 0):**\n  - A \"MASHED!\" pop-up, a confetti burst in the player's colour, `pose: 'die'` then `down: 1`, and a Lance quip.\n  - −1 life. The weapon and add-ons reset.\n  - 2.0 s later the contestant **drops in on a cable** from the ceiling, under a spotlight near the entry door: z 70 → 0\n    over 0.5 s. It has 2.5 s invulnerability and at least 1 Showstopper.\n- **Out of lives (co-op):** state `out`, HUD \"OUT — PRESS START TO CONTINUE\".\n  - Pressing Start (Options/Enter) brings the contestant back with full lives and **half their winnings** (the producer's\n    cut). The run keeps going for the others.\n  - When **every** contestant is out at once, the global CONTINUE countdown runs (§5.10).\n- **Slide** (`slide`, pressed): 0.28 s at `slideSpeed` **(T)** 235 u/s in the move direction (or the facing if standing).\n  - I-frames for the first 0.22 s. It can fire while sliding.\n  - Cooldown `slideCooldown` **(T)** 0.85 s. Dust puff and a whoosh.\n  - The rig rolls: pass `flip: u` (0→1 over the slide).\n- **Showstopper** (`bomb`, pressed, stock > 0; max 3; 2 at the start of a run):\n  - A 0.12 s white `game.flash` (it follows the comfort settings), a camera-flashbulb burst sound and a boom.\n  - Ring particles and `particles.glints` everywhere.\n  - Every enemy on screen takes 25 damage; boss parts take 30. All foe bullets are cleared, and enemies are stunned for 1.0 s.\n  - Ratings +8. A 0.08 s `game.freeze` (not in sessions).\n- **Showboat** (`showboat`, pressed, 3 s cooldown): a 0.6 s pose. Cycle `pose: 'cheer' | 'hips' | 'wave'` with\n  `expr: 'smile'`; no moving or firing.\n  - When it completes, let n = enemies within 64 units and m = enemies within 32. Ratings gain\n    `4 + 3n + 3m` (cap 30), and a pop-up \"STYLE +N\".\n  - Crowd cheer, louder with the gain. A hit during the pose cancels the gain.\n  - This is how the show's theme becomes a risk/reward mechanic.\n- **Walk-in:** at room `ENTER`, contestants walk from the entry door to a 2×2 formation 40 units inside. They are\n  invulnerable and ignore input for 1.2 s.\n- **Local drop-in:** `game.allowJoin({ max: 4, keys: false, onJoin })` stays open on every play scene. A joining pad gets\n  the next free slot, colour and look, $0, default lives, and drops in on a cable.\n\n### 5.2 Weapons and power-ups\n\n| id | On-screen name | Kind | Behaviour | Duration (T: `puDuration` ×) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `blaster` | STUDIO BLASTER | primary (default) | 9 shots/s (`fireRate` T), bolt dmg 1 (`dmgMul` T), speed 330 (`bulletSpeed` T), r 2.5, life 0.9 s | ∞ |\n| `spread` | TRIPLE PLAY | primary | 8/s, 3 bolts at −13°/0°/+13°, dmg 1 each | 14 s |\n| `rockets` | PRIME-TIME ROCKETS | primary | 3.5/s, a rocket accelerating 180→300, direct dmg 4 + splash 3 (r 24), smoke trail, explodes on a hit, a wall or end of life (0.9 s) | 12 s |\n| `popcorn` | POPCORN POPPER | primary | 2.5/s, a lobbed bag (`E.arc`) landing 90 ahead (or at the reticle if closer), splash 2 (r 18) + 6 kernels outward (dmg 1.5, speed 200, life 0.35, pierce 1). Clears crowds behind cover. | 12 s |\n| `laser` | LIMELIGHT LASER | primary | a continuous beam from the muzzle to the first wall, piercing every enemy on the line, 16 DPS (`16*dt` per step), turn rate limited to 7 rad/s, a hum loop | 10 s |\n| `records` | GOLD RECORDS | add-on | 2 gold discs orbit at r 26, 4.2 rad/s; each touch does 2 dmg (0.15 s cooldown per enemy) and destroys small foe bullets (orb, shard, coin, card, static) | 15 s |\n| `shield` | SPONSOR SHIELD | add-on | invulnerable bubble with a flickering \"KRUNCH-O\" logo; touching enemies take 1 dmg per 0.2 s | 8 s |\n| `speed` | SPEED SNEAKERS | add-on | move ×1.45, slide cooldown ×0.6, afterimages (`r.actor(..., { ghost })`) | 10 s |\n| `bomb` | SHOWSTOPPER | instant | +1 stock (at max 3: +$2,000 instead) | — |\n| `life` | ENCORE | instant | +1 life | — |\n\n- One **primary** slot. A new primary replaces the old one; the same primary refreshes its timer. Add-ons stack with the\n  primary and with each other.\n- In the HUD, the weapon icon has a draining bar. The last 2 s blink with a tick. At expiry a small \"BACK TO BASICS\".\n- **Projectile tech:**\n  - Blaster and spread bolts, the kernels and every foe bullet: `E.Bullets` pools on the `'ground'` plane, `pShots`\n    (team `p`, `data: { owner, w }`) and `fShots` (team `f`, `data: { id, kind }`; ids are needed for netplay).\n  - Rockets and popcorn bags are small custom projectile lists (update, draw and explode).\n  - The laser is a per-step segment test against enemy circles; its length comes from stepping the ray against\n    `map.heightAt` (or `map.ray`).\n- Player bullets stop at walls and closed doors (`shots.update(dt, map)`).\n\n### 5.3 Pickups and economy\n\n| Pickup | Look (string sprite, ~10 px, bobbing, glow) | Effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| Bill | green banknote | $100 |\n| Stack | banded stack | $500 |\n| Gold bar | gold ingot with glint | $1,000 |\n| Briefcase | silver case with \"$\" | $5,000 |\n| Prize box | gift box (red, blue or gold, with a bow) | a named prize from the episode's table (§7.6), worth its value |\n| Golden Key | gold key, star glint | team key +1 (out of 9) |\n| Capsule | a mini CRT TV whose screen shows the power-up icon and its letter (S spread, R rockets, P popcorn, L laser, G gold records, B shield bubble, F fast sneakers) | the power-up |\n| Showstopper | a flashbulb | +1 bomb |\n| Encore | a gold star with \"1UP\" | +1 life |\n| Golden Statuette | a big gold \"LANCIE\" award statue | Green Room grand prize (ends the bonus) |\n\n- **Collection:** a contestant touches it (radius = contestant r + 7).\n  - Cash and prizes are credited × the ratings multiplier × `cashMul` **(T)**, to the collector only.\n  - A pop-up \"+$1,500\" in the collector's colour, plus \"ka-ching\".\n- **Lifetime:** 9 s on the floor, blinking for the last 2.5 s. Keys, placed items and the statuette never expire.\n- **Magnet:** once a room is clear, cash within 60 units drifts to the nearest contestant.\n- **Prize box:**\n  - Draws the next prize from the episode table, shuffled with the run's seed, no repeats until it runs out.\n  - A banner: \"YOU WON: A SELF-AWARE TOASTER! ($2,500)\", with Penny's squeal (babble).\n  - The prize joins that player's recap list.\n- **Keys** are shared by the team. A banner \"GOLDEN KEY 4/9\" and a chime; Lance's key line the first time.\n- **Drops** on enemy death roll the enemy's drop table (§5.8) × `dropMul` **(T)**. Floor caps: at most 3 capsules and\n  25 cash pickups at once.\n- **Prize crates** are placed props: HP 3, solid while whole (`map.solid`, or a static body). When broken they spill\n  3–5 cash, a 50 % chance of a prize, and the key if the room's data says so.\n\n### 5.4 Ratings (the show's heartbeat)\n\n- `R` runs 0–100 and is **shared by the team**: it's the show's rating, not a player's. It's drawn top-centre as a big TV\n  \"RATINGS\" gauge with a needle, the multiplier and an APPLAUSE light.\n- **Gains:**\n  - A kill adds the enemy's `rat` value. A multi-kill adds +2 for each kill past 2 within 0.4 s.\n  - Showboat (§5.1).\n  - A **near miss**: +0.5 when a foe bullet passes within 7 units of a contestant without hitting (at most 3 a second).\n  - A prize +2, a key +5, a boss part destroyed +10.\n  - All gains × `ratingsGain` **(T)**.\n- **Losses:** a hit −12; a death −25; BANKRUPT (the wheel) −10.\n- **Decay:** after 2.5 s without a gain, R falls by `ratingsDecay` **(T)** 2.5 a second.\n- **Multiplier:** R < 20 ×1; < 40 ×2; < 60 ×3; < 80 ×4; ≥ 80 ×5.\n- **PRIME TIME** when R reaches 100. It lasts `primeTime` **(T)** 12 s:\n  - The multiplier is locked at ×5.\n  - A \"PRIME TIME!\" banner and fanfare sting; `audio.layer('prime', 1, .3)` brings the brass in.\n  - The audience throws 3 random capsules in arcs from the bleachers; confetti cannons fire; the APPLAUSE sign blinks.\n  - Afterwards R = 60, with a 20 s cooldown before the next Prime Time.\n- **RATINGS STUNT** (anti-stall): if R < 5 for 10 s during waves, \"WE'RE LOSING VIEWERS!\" and the producers release the\n  episode's stunt from a random door:\n  - Ep1: 12 clapbots.\n  - Ep2: a dolly tank.\n  - Ep3: a mascot brute.\n\n  R +15. At most once per room.\n\n### 5.5 Arena, doors and hazards\n\n**Geometry, the same in every room.** A TileMap of 23 × 15 cells is 368 × 240 world units.\n- **Walls:** row 0, row 14, col 0 and col 22, wall type 1: `h: 26`, studio-flat faces (§9.2).\n  - The south wall row is `cut: true`, so it never hides the floor from the camera.\n- **Interior:** 21 × 13 cells, x 16–352, y 16–224, centre **(184, 120)**.\n- **Door gaps** (3 cells, wall type 2, painted by our door painter):\n  - N: cols 10–12 of row 0. S: cols 10–12 of row 14. W: rows 6–8 of col 0. E: rows 6–8 of col 22.\n  - Door centres: N (184, 8), S (184, 232), W (8, 120), E (360, 120).\n- **Room data uses interior cells** `[cx, cy]`, cx 0–20 and cy 0–12. In world units that's\n  `(24 + cx * 16, 24 + cy * 16)`.\n\n**Door states:**\n- `closed`.\n- `opening`: 0.35 s; the shutter rolls up, a red beacon and a klaxon.\n- `open`: spawning.\n- `closing`: 0.3 s; a slam and dust.\n- `exit`: a green beacon and a lit sign above the door with the destination's name and room-type icon. Its cells become\n  walkable (`map.set(cx, cy, 0)`), with an exit trigger 10 units past the threshold.\n\n**Spawning:**\n- From a **door:** enemies appear 20 units outside the gap and walk in with wall collision off (\"entering\") until inside\n  the interior. The door stays open while its queue has spawns, plus 0.6 s.\n- From the **audience** (hecklers): a leap from the E or W bleachers over the wall. An arc from x −30 or 398 to a random\n  interior point on that side; 0.7 s, invulnerable in the air.\n- **Drop:** from the ceiling at a random free interior point at least 50 units from every contestant. A shadow\n  telegraphs for 0.6 s, then it falls.\n\n**Hazards:**\n- **Conveyor belts** (Ep2): strips 2 cells tall across the room. Each pushes everything on it (contestants, enemies,\n  pickups, the key) at ±40 u/s, with animated chevrons. Direction reverses every 12 s after a 1.5 s warning (lights, an\n  alarm, the belt slowing).\n- **Pyro vents** (Ep3): floor grates firing in data-driven patterns.\n  - Telegraph: 1.0 s of glow, sparks and rumble.\n  - Then a 1.2 s flame column, r 12: 1 damage to contestants, 3 damage a second to enemies. It's emissive and casts light.\n- **Tomato splats:** puddles of r 14 for 6 s that slow contestants to ×0.6.\n- **Prize crates** and **vault locks:** static targets (rooms 1-6, 2-6, 3-4).\n\n**Camera:**\n- Fixed: `game.focus(184, 120, 0)` every update.\n- Zoom is fitted so the walled arena fills the screen, leaving ~22 px top and bottom for the HUD. Measure\n  `map.bounds(game.view)` at zoom 1 and recompute when the screen changes size (`game.screen.listeners`). Clamp to\n  [0.6, 1.6], then × `camZoom` **(T)**.\n- The scene's `camera: { zoom: false }` turns the player's zoom off. Shake goes through `game.shake` (comfort settings\n  apply).\n- Wide or tall screens show the studio around the arena (bleachers, cranes, the jumbotron; §9.2).\n\n### 5.6 Room flow (phases)\n\n`ENTER` → `INTRO` → `WAVES` → `CLEAR` → `EXIT` → `TRANSITION` → the next room's `ENTER`.\n\n- **ENTER** (1.2 s): the walk-in. The door behind the contestants slams.\n- **INTRO** (≤ 2 s): Lance's room line on the jumbotron and a \"MASH!\" stinger. An episode's first room is preceded by the\n  episode card (§11.3).\n- **WAVES:** the room script (§5.7) runs.\n- **CLEAR:** every wave has spawned and no counted enemy is alive. Mimes, crates and vault locks don't count.\n  - \"ROOM CLEAR!\" banner and sting.\n  - Each living contestant gets a clear bonus of `$1,000 × episode × multiplier`, +50 % if cleared under the room's\n    `par` seconds.\n  - A Lance line. Remaining foe bullets fade out.\n- **EXIT:** the exit doors open with their signs. The first contestant into an exit trigger picks it; 0.8 s later every\n  contestant goes. Pickups can still be collected meanwhile.\n  - If nobody leaves within 25 s: \"HURRY UP!\" and 4 clapbots every 6 s from the doors that aren't exits.\n- **TRANSITION:** a 0.4 s TV-static wipe, then the \"NEXT ON MASH TV\" map card (2.5 s, skippable with confirm), then\n  another static wipe into the next room.\n\n**Boss rooms:** `ENTER` → `BOSS_INTRO` (~4 s cutscene, skippable) → `BOSS` → `BOSS_DEATH` (3 s) → `CLEAR` → the `recap`\nscene.\n\n**Survive rooms** (1-4): `WAVES` ends when the timer runs out and the field is clear.\n\n### 5.7 Waves: the room script data format\n\n```js\n// src/20-content-*.js — one room\n{ id: '1-2', name: 'MIME FIELD', ep: 1, type: 'battle', cell: [0, 2], exits: { N: '1-4' }, par: 60,\n  place: [['mime', 4, 3], ['mime', 10, 2] /* … */],      // [kind, cx, cy] at room start (interior cells)\n  items: [[1.5, 'spread', [5, 6]]],                      // [seconds after WAVES starts, pickup, 'center' | 'drop' | [cx, cy]]\n  key: { rule: 'mimes' },                                 // 'mimes' | 'place' | 'crate' | 'carrier' | 'vault' | 'final' | 'survive'\n  hazards: [],                                            // e.g. { kind: 'conveyor', rows: [3, 6, 9], dirs: [1, -1, 1] }\n  waves: [\n    { at: 2, spawn: [['S', 'goon', 8]] },\n    { left: 3, at: 8, spawn: [['N', 'popper', 2], ['E', 'goon', 8]] },\n    { left: 3, at: 10, spawn: [['W', 'goon', 10]], items: [['rockets', 'drop']] },\n  ] }\n```\n\n- **When a wave starts:** `at` seconds after the previous wave started, or when no more than `left` counted enemies are\n  alive, whichever comes first. With only one of them given, that one alone. Build it as one `game.script` per room:","numLines":420,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2017}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DvLMx5xvhje5uBWrnZJirF","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
