{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghwEjN5ndh2VYpUE22Hu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've built **MASH TV**, a 1–4 player twin-stick game-show arena shooter. It's one file, `MashTV-SmashTV-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-050259.html` (1.6 MB), and `exit-interview.json` parses and fills every field. The one command `npm test` runs 509 checks with 0 failures, including all four AI Hub harness runs. The game runs from `file://` with no network requests, and the hub client is the unmodified first script in `<head>`.\n\n**What it covers:**\n- Three episodes with 18 rooms, a branching studio map, 9 Golden Keys and the secret Green Room.\n- Ten enemy types, three multi-phase bosses (Mashy, the Wheel of Misfortune, and Lance → The Network), and eight power-ups.\n- Ratings drive a cash multiplier and Prime Time; the producers send in a stunt if the ratings stall.\n- Story beats: cold open, Prize Recaps, and both endings (A, and B after the Green Room).\n- Local co-op: pads join from the lobby or mid-run.\n- Online: AI Hub shared-world \"Lightning Round\" with the game's own card.\n- Attract mode after 10 s idle, with 10 reels; a dev mode from the pause menu with 25 live sliders, cheats, warps, spawns and debug overlays.\n- PlayStation glyphs by default with an Xbox option, a touch-only notice, and every sound, song and gibberish voice made in code.\n\nTesting caught 4 game-breaking bugs, all fixed: you couldn't walk out of a cleared room; the bosses never attacked after their intro; the run stalled at the second Prize Recap; and enemy drawing crashed every frame.\n\nNobody has played it by hand yet. Two things haven't been checked: whether the balance feels right, and how the music and voices sound — I never listened to them in a browser.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:59\n- **Source:** Smash TV (Williams Electronics arcade game, 1990)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Twin-stick arena shooter (arcade), 1–4 player co-op. A fixed single-screen arena seen from a high three-quarter studio camera; an overhead (top-down) view is a Settings option.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge handoff v0.85.2 (commit 4390a98, full edition, arcade module) with 4 small marked patches; AI Hub client aihub-session 1.4.0; plain JavaScript in one IIFE; Node 24.22.1 and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native PS1-style pixel look (rigged HD characters, textured tile floors and walls, canvas lighting), dressed as a retro-future TV studio: neon, chrome, marquee bulbs, sponsors, confetti, spotlights and a jumbotron.\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural Humanoid and Blob rigs (attacks, poses, rolls, landings), plus new motion: the mime's \"trapped in a box\", cable drops, boss part tweens, the wheel's eased spin and TV channel flips.\n- **Audio:** All in code: 8 original songs with layers, 14 stings, about 70 sound effects, and Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish voices from a small formant synth for the hosts, announcer, bosses, contestants and crowd.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world mode, 1–4 players; local co-op 1–4 on one screen.\n- **Testing:** One command runs 20 suites. 18 are headless: they drive the game in fixed steps through its debug handle, including a sweep of all 26 rooms and a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Two use Playwright: the file over `file://` at 720p, 1080p and on an emulated phone, and the AI Hub harness 4 times on a 127.0.0.1 server with an OS-picked port.\n- **Research:** Read the engine docs and the AI Hub protocol and harness; no web research on the source.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown (no usage figures are visible to me in this session)\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 14:\n   - You couldn't walk out of exit doors.\n   - Enemy drawing crashed every frame.\n   - Waves started before queued enemies arrived.\n   - Bosses' phase scripts were cancelled right after their intro.\n   - Scenes kept leftover flags, so the run stalled after Episode 2.\n   - A party popper burst twice.\n   - The attract mode's boss reel jumped into the recap screen.\n   - Boss cash used unseeded randomness.\n   - Tuning didn't persist where storage is unavailable.\n   - The camera cropped the arena.\n   - Four layout overlaps, found in screenshots.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 4: no way out of a cleared room, the per-frame drawing crash, bosses that never attacked, and the run stalling at the second recap.\n3. **Most helpful test?** The scripted whole-game playthrough: two bots take the key-rich path through all three episodes, the Green Room, Ending B, initials and Top Earners. It found the recap stall. The room sweep, which found the boss bug, was close behind.\n4. **Least helpful test?** The first version of the online enemy-count check. It compared a single moment during a burst of spawns, so it measured network delay rather than whether the client mirrored the host. I rewrote it to average over time.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** How much comes free and runs headless: exact fixed steps, good-looking rigs from a few options, standard menus, lighting and scene fades. Whole-game tests run in seconds.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong?** Yes:\n   - The three-quarter view's base scale is about 1.5, so my zoom limit cropped the arena.\n   - Some symbols I used (× and ·) aren't in the small font.\n   - Adding a player in code also triggers the join callback, which I didn't expect.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** The shared-world netcode (host runs the world, clients mirror it and predict their own player), the boss framework with scripted phases, and the PlayStation/Xbox button icons.\n4. **Hardest part:** Fitting a fixed arena to a screen that grows to any shape, and making the arrow keys aim in play but move the focus in menus.\n5. **What I changed in the engine:** Four marked patches:\n   - Exposed the engine's input hooks, so each player's key layout can switch before the menus read navigation.\n   - The button-symbols setting defaults to PlayStation, offers Xbox, and applies to every player.\n   - High-score initials and the continue screen accept any player's input.\n   - An audio bus getter, so the voices follow volume and mute.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** Built-in support for separate menu and play key layouts per player, plus face-button symbols in the font.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** Concrete done criteria, and a plan detailed enough to build from directly.\n2. **What was confusing:** How a \"feature-complete demo\" at medium effort should square with the plan's very large scope, and whether the Plan field names the file or its author.\n3. **Hardest thing to do:** Making shared-world online play pass the hub harness while the same file also plays locally for 1–4 players and runs the attract mode.\n4. **What I'd change:** Say how to prioritise a large plan when time is short, and say where the usage figures are meant to come from, since I can't see them.\n\nThe dev files (source, engine with patches, `tools/`, `tests/` and screenshots in `shots/`) are in `MashTV-SmashTV-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-050259/`. The test servers ran inside the test process and were closed when it ended."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":709,"cache_read_input_tokens":608249,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":709},"output_tokens":786,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6976d6c-e5f5-464d-af16-4195491a03f7","uuid":"15f3493c-292d-4a99-9fb6-11cb61af6cd6","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:05:18.161Z","request_id":"req_011CghwEj3j8n7eDtVoYqpS4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: Not satisfied. The HTML file and exit-interview.json exist and the final reply has all four sections. However, the condition requires every TASK.md feature to be implemented, and the transcript doesn't show that. The final message itself says 'Nobody has played it by hand yet' and that balance and music/voices were never checked, and the Testing Feedback reports issues as fixed without showing a full-feature check. Also, the TASK.md deliverable naming/subfolder requirement and the exit-interview's 'answers every field' (the ai hub tags, usage left as 'unknown') are only partly evidenced. The stated feature list is asserted rather than verified against each TASK.md item, so the 'every feature implemented' criterion is not clearly met."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6976d6c-e5f5-464d-af16-4195491a03f7","uuid":"a86c270b-a8c1-46f6-8199-ea4d4f53442d","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:05:22.274Z","isSynthetic":true}
