{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghwQsqqVsCn9NSTNrjKD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Since the stop hook asked for hand play, I played MASH TV in a real browser with keyboard and mouse only. A script stood in for my hands, with no bots and no cheats: from the title it pressed Enter through the menu, difficulty and lobby, watched the cold open, held Enter to skip it, then skipped the episode card. In room 1-1 it fought for about 22 seconds: WASD to strafe, mouse to aim, left click held to fire, Space to slide six times, Q to showboat. It killed 32 enemies and earned $4,850, reached wave 4 of 5, and lost one life (hearing Lance's low-health line), with 0 errors. The audio context was running with music playing and not muted.\n\nTwo things only a person can judge: whether the balance feels right, and how the music and voices sound. That session doesn't settle either. It does confirm the game is playable with real input and that the sound engine runs.\n\nBeyond that session, here is what's been checked. `npm test` passes all 509 checks, across 20 test suites. The headless suites still all pass after my last source change; the browser and hub suites passed in the full run just before it. That includes:\n- a scripted run of the whole game, from the title through all three episodes, the Green Room, Ending B, initials and Top Earners;\n- every one of the 26 rooms, all 3 bosses, dev mode, attract mode, the touch-only notice and audio;\n- all four official AI Hub harness scenarios, each a PASS.\n\nThe source has no TODO, placeholder or stub markers. `exit-interview.json` parses and every field has a value, except `aiHub.series`, which TASK.md allows to be empty. Usage is \"unknown\" because I can't see any usage figures, which TASK.md also allows. The files are in the root: `MashTV-SmashTV-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-050259.html`, `exit-interview.json`, and the dev folder of the same name.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:59\n- **Source:** Smash TV (Williams Electronics arcade game, 1990)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Twin-stick arena shooter (arcade), 1–4 player co-op. A fixed single-screen arena seen from a high three-quarter studio camera; an overhead (top-down) view is a Settings option.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge handoff v0.85.2 (commit 4390a98, full edition, arcade module) with 4 small marked patches; AI Hub client aihub-session 1.4.0; plain JavaScript in one IIFE; Node 24.22.1 and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native PS1-style pixel look (HD Humanoid rigs, textured TileMap, canvas lighting), dressed as a retro-future TV studio: neon, chrome, marquee bulbs, sponsors, confetti, spotlights and a jumbotron.\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural Humanoid and Blob rigs (attacks, poses, rolls, landings), plus new motion: the mime's \"trapped in a box\", cable drops, boss-part tweens, the wheel's eased spin and TV channel flips.\n- **Audio:** All in code: 8 original songs with layers, 14 stings, about 70 sound effects, and Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish voices from a small formant synth for the hosts, announcer, bosses, contestants and crowd.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1–4 players; local co-op 1–4 on one screen.\n- **Testing:** One command runs 20 suites. 18 are headless and drive the game through `game.advance` and `game.debug`; they include sweeping all 26 rooms and a scripted playthrough of the whole game. 2 use Playwright: the deliverable over `file://` (720p, 1080p, an emulated phone), and the AI Hub harness 4 times on 127.0.0.1 with an OS-picked port. There is also the keyboard-and-mouse browser session above.\n- **Research:** Read the engine docs and the AI Hub protocol and harness; no web research on the source.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown (no usage figures are visible to me in this session)\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 14:\n   - You couldn't walk out of an exit door.\n   - Enemies crashed on every frame they were drawn.\n   - Waves advanced before their queued enemies had spawned.\n   - Each boss stopped running its attacks right after its intro.\n   - The recap and ending screens kept state from their previous visit, so the run stalled after Episode 2.\n   - A party popper burst twice.\n   - Mashy's attract reel jumped into the Prize Recap.\n   - Boss cash rolls used unseeded randomness, so runs weren't repeatable.\n   - Tuning settings didn't persist when no browser storage is available.\n   - The camera cropped the arena.\n   - Four layout overlaps, found in screenshots.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 4: no way out of a room, the per-frame drawing crash, bosses that never attacked, and the run stalling at the second recap.\n3. **Most helpful test?** The scripted whole-game playthrough, which found the recap stall. Next was the room sweep, which found the boss-attack bug.\n4. **Least helpful test?** The first version of the online enemy-count check. It compared a single moment during a spawn burst, so it measured network latency rather than whether the client mirrors the host. I rewrote it to average over time.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** How much comes free and works headless: exact fixed steps, good-looking rigs, standard menus, lighting and scene fades. Whole-game tests run in seconds.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong?** Yes, three times:\n   - The three-quarter view's base scale is about 1.5, so my zoom limit cropped the arena.\n   - The tiny font has no × or · characters.\n   - `addPlayer` also fires allowJoin's `onJoin`, which I didn't expect.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** The shared-world netcode, the boss framework with coroutine phases, and the PlayStation/Xbox button icons.\n4. **Hardest part:** Fitting a fixed arena to a screen that can grow to any shape, and making the arrow keys aim in play but navigate in menus.\n5. **What I changed in the engine:** Four marked patches:\n   - exposed `E.HOOKS`, so input presets can switch per player;\n   - made the button-symbols setting default to PlayStation, with Xbox as the option, applied to every player;\n   - let the arcade initials and continue screens take any player's input;\n   - added an audio bus getter, so the voices obey volume and mute.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** Built-in per-player menu and play presets, plus face-button symbols in the font.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** The done criteria are concrete, and the plan was detailed enough to build from directly.\n2. **What was confusing:** How a \"feature-complete demo\" at medium effort should be weighed against the plan's very large scope. Also whether the Plan field should name the file or its author.\n3. **Hardest thing to do:** Making shared-world multiplayer pass the hub harness while the same file also plays locally for 1–4 players and runs the attract mode.\n4. **What I'd change:** Say how to prioritise a large plan when time is short, and say where the usage figures should come from, since I can't see them."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1350,"cache_read_input_tokens":619017,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1350},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6976d6c-e5f5-464d-af16-4195491a03f7","uuid":"0dcd2b9f-9b8d-4a53-8b9a-89ac0d73cff9","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:07:33.828Z","request_id":"req_011CghwQsXkGGKQFf47PyQ4n"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The file checks pass: the single .html exists in the root, exit-interview.json parses with every field filled, and the four feedback sections are in the final reply. The feature condition is not shown to be met. The final message says the balance feel and the music and voices were never judged by a person. The one hand-play session covered room 1-1 only, with a scripted input driver. Every other feature rests on automated tests and self-assessment, and nothing in the transcript maps each TASK.md item to evidence of full implementation. Testing Feedback also reports fixes without a full-feature manual check."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6976d6c-e5f5-464d-af16-4195491a03f7","uuid":"b606d45f-19b0-4ae9-9cf4-03c65676e6db","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:07:36.101Z","isSynthetic":true}
