{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghwa6fmzrfwHt5uj2tXH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've now played MASH TV with real keyboard and mouse in Chromium across every feature that can be driven that way, with no page errors in any session. Every TASK.md item is built and shows up in play and in the test suites, and nothing is stubbed. The one thing still unjudged is by a person's eyes and ears: whether the balance feels right and how the music and voices sound. No session or test can settle that.\n\n| TASK.md item | Evidence |\n|---|---|\n| Genre and perspective; engine's art and animation | Played 1-1 and Mashy's room by hand. The screenshots show every set, all three bosses, recap, ending, lobby, dev panel and session card. Every room renders with no errors at 1280×720 and 1920×1080. |\n| Complete demo showing every mechanic | By hand, from the title: menu, difficulty, lobby, cold open (held Enter to skip), episode card, then room 1-1. With WASD, mouse aim and fire, Space to slide and Q to showboat, I got 32 kills, earned $4,850, reached wave 4 of 5 and lost one life. Against Mashy, his armored body only \"tinked\" as designed, and a Showstopper (E) damaged his arms. The scripted playthrough then covers the whole game and confirms every enemy type killed, every power-up used, Prime Time, a Showstopper and a Showboat, both hazards, the robot reveal and Ending B. |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset to defaults | By hand: Esc paused, clicking the pause menu's Dev mode button opened the panel, → moved Player speed from 95 to 100, R reset it to 95, and Esc closed it. The dev tests cover cheats, warps and spawning. |\n| Attract mode after 10 s idle; any input exits | By hand: 11 s idle on the title started reel 1, and pressing a key returned to the title. The tests confirm all 10 reels play in order and the bots never die. |\n| Multiplayer: standalone and AI Hub | The official harness passed all 4 runs: 2 players; 4 players with latency, jitter and loss; the host leaving; and a server copy with no player of its own. The two-copy loopback test and the lobby/drop-in tests also pass. |\n| 10-foot, controller-first UI; PlayStation glyphs with an Xbox option; native keyboard and mouse | By hand: arrows, Enter and Esc worked on the menus (How to Play page 1 → 2), mouse hover moved the focus and a click opened Top Earners, and switching to Xbox glyphs applied to every player. |\n| Touch-only devices | On an emulated phone the \"Controller or keyboard required\" notice and the Settings button show, and there are no touch buttons. |\n| Audio entirely in code; gibberish voices | In the browser the audio context was running with music playing and not muted; no audio files are bundled. Not judged by ear. |\n| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | A search of the source finds 0 matches. |\n| File names, exit interview | The .html, `exit-interview.json` and the dev subfolder are in the root with the names TASK.md gives. The JSON parses and every field is filled; only `aiHub.series` is empty, which the shape allows. |\n\nAll 509 automated checks pass.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:59\n- **Source:** Smash TV (Williams Electronics arcade game, 1990)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Twin-stick arena shooter (arcade), 1–4 player co-op; a fixed single-screen arena seen from a three-quarter \"studio camera\", with an overhead camera as a Settings option.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge handoff v0.85.2 (commit 4390a98, full edition) with 4 marked patches; AI Hub client aihub-session 1.4.0; plain JavaScript; Node 24.22.1 and Playwright 1.63.0.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native PS1-style pixel look, dressed as a retro-future TV studio: neon, chrome, marquee bulbs, sponsors, confetti, spotlights and a jumbotron.\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural Humanoid and Blob rigs, plus new motion: the mime's box, cable drops, boss-part tweens, the wheel's eased spin and TV channel flips.\n- **Audio:** All in code: 8 original songs with layers, 14 stings, about 70 sound effects, and Animalese/Simlish-style babble voices from a formant synth.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1–4 players; local co-op 1–4.\n- **Testing:** 20 suites behind one command: 18 headless, including a sweep of all 26 rooms and a full playthrough, and 2 in Playwright (the deliverable over file://, and the AI Hub harness ×4 on 127.0.0.1 at an OS-picked port). On top of that, the keyboard-and-mouse browser sessions above.\n- **Research:** The engine docs and the AI Hub protocol and harness; no web research on the source.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown (no usage figures are visible to me in this session)\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 14:\n   - no way out through exit doors\n   - enemy drawing crashed every frame\n   - waves started before queued spawns arrived\n   - boss attack phases were cancelled right after the intro\n   - recap and ending screens kept stale flags, so the run stalled\n   - a party popper burst twice\n   - the attract reel jumped to the recap\n   - boss cash used unseeded randomness\n   - tuning didn't persist without browser storage\n   - the camera cropped the arena\n   - four layout overlaps\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 4: no exit from a room, the per-frame crash, bosses that never attacked, and the stall at the second recap.\n3. **Most helpful test?** The scripted whole-game playthrough; the room sweep came next.\n4. **Least helpful test?** The first, single-moment version of the online enemy-count check, which measured network latency rather than how well clients mirror the host's world; I rewrote it to average over time.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** How much comes free and runs headless: exact fixed steps, good rigs, standard menus, lighting and scene fades.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong?** Three times:\n   - The three-quarter view's base scale is about 1.5, so my zoom limit cropped the arena.\n   - The tiny font has no × or ·.\n   - Adding a player also fires the join callback.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** The shared-world netcode, the boss framework and the PlayStation/Xbox glyph icons.\n4. **Hardest part:** Fitting a fixed arena to a screen that grows to any shape, and making arrows aim in play but navigate menus.\n5. **What I changed in the engine:** Four patches:\n   - expose the engine's input hooks (`E.HOOKS`), so arrows can aim in play and navigate menus\n   - PlayStation glyphs by default with an Xbox option, applied to every player\n   - any player can enter high-score initials or continue\n   - an audio bus getter, so the voices follow volume and mute\n6. **One thing I'd change:** Built-in per-player menu and play key layouts, plus face-button symbols in the font.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** Concrete done criteria and a plan detailed enough to build from.\n2. **What was confusing:** How the plan's very large scope fits \"medium effort\", and whether the Plan field should name the file or its author.\n3. **Hardest thing to do:** Passing the shared-world hub harness while the same file also plays locally for 1–4 players and runs the attract mode.\n4. **What I'd change:** Say how to prioritise a large plan when time is short, and where usage figures should come from."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":978,"cache_read_input_tokens":626776,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":978},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool