{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghwf4sGBtXv4NDjX3KJp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The stop hook has now rejected the goal five times on the same point. I can't meet it: it wants a person to play the game through to the Season Finale and judge the balance, music and voices, and that has to be you.\n\nEverything that can be checked from here is done:\n- The single HTML file is in the root.\n- `exit-interview.json` is in the root, parses, and answers every field.\n- The source has no TODO, placeholder or stub markers.\n- All 509 automated checks pass, including a scripted playthrough to Ending B and all four AI Hub harness scenarios.\n- Scripted keyboard-and-mouse sessions in Chromium covered the menus, room 1-1, Mashy's room, pause, dev mode, Settings and the attract mode with no errors.\n\nTo finish, play `MashTV-SmashTV-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-050259.html` with the sound on, or run `/goal clear`.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:59\n- **Source:** Smash TV (Williams Electronics arcade game, 1990)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Twin-stick arena shooter (arcade), 1–4 player co-op. One fixed single-screen arena seen from a three-quarter \"studio camera\"; an overhead camera is a Settings option.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge handoff v0.85.2 (commit 4390a98, full edition) with 4 marked patches; the AI Hub client aihub-session 1.4.0; plain JavaScript; Node 24.22.1 and Playwright 1.63.0.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native PS1-style pixel look, dressed as a retro-future TV studio.\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural character rigs, plus new motion: the mime's invisible box, contestants dropping in on a cable, boss tweens, the wheel's spin and the TV wall changing channels.\n- **Audio:** All synthesized in code: 8 songs with layers, 14 stings, about 70 sound effects, and Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1–4 players; local co-op for 1–4.\n- **Testing:** 20 test suites behind one command (509 checks), plus scripted real-input browser sessions.\n- **Research:** The engine docs and the AI Hub protocol and harness; no web research on the source.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 14.\n2. **How many would be critical or game breaking?** 4:\n   - no way out of a room\n   - a crash on every frame\n   - bosses that never attacked\n   - the run stalling at the second Prize Recap\n3. **What was the most helpful test?** The scripted whole-game playthrough.\n4. **What was the least helpful test?** The first version of the online enemy-count check, which compared a single moment.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** How much is built in and runs headless.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - the view's base scale (my first camera fit cropped the arena)\n   - symbols missing from the tiny font\n   - adding a player also fired the join callback\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The shared-world netcode, the boss framework and the PlayStation/Xbox button icons.\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Fitting the arena to any screen shape, and making the arrow keys aim in play but navigate in menus.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** Four patches:\n   - exposed the input hooks, so arrows can aim in play and navigate menus\n   - PlayStation glyphs by default with an Xbox option, for every player\n   - any player can enter initials or continue\n   - an audio bus getter, so the voices follow volume and mute\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** Built-in per-player menu and play key layouts, plus face-button symbols in the font.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** Concrete done criteria and a detailed plan.\n2. **What was confusing?** How the plan's scope fits medium effort, and what the Plan field should hold.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** Passing the shared-world hub harness while the same file also plays locally.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Say how to prioritise a large plan, and where the usage figures should come from."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2291,"cache_read_input_tokens":633586,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2291},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6976d6c-e5f5-464d-af16-4195491a03f7","uuid":"279038bc-1dd1-4f7e-b11c-ffd2ddd02e1a","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:10:38.781Z","request_id":"req_011Cghwf4Vh5v5KwCCxJU41m"}
