{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgiEMndTmAZoAdE1FigsK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"MASH TV is finished: the single HTML file and `exit-interview.json` are in the root, and the final build passes all 510 checks. That includes the browser checks and all four AI Hub harness scenarios.\n\n**Full playthrough with real input.** I drove the whole game in Chromium using only keyboard and mouse events, on Rookie with no cheats. A script decided the moves and turned them into keypresses and mouse moves; nothing reached into the game directly. It went from the title through the lobby, the cold open and all three episodes:\n- It beat Mashy, the Wheel of Misfortune, and Lance → The Network, including the robot reveal.\n- It saw all three Prize Recaps, Ending A and the credits.\n- It took 13.6 minutes, with 0 continues and 0 errors, and finished on $1,499,720.\n- It killed every enemy type, used all 7 power-ups, and the audio engine was running throughout.\n\nThe run collected only 3 of the 9 keys, so it got Ending A. The Green Room and Ending B are covered by the scripted test playthrough instead.\n\n**What that run caught.** Two real problems that the bot-driven tests missed, both now fixed:\n- A disco ball could stick in a corner forever, so its room could never be cleared. There's now a regression check for it.\n- The boss health bar covered Mashy's head; it's now at the bottom of the screen.\n\nThe run also exposed a bug in my test script's mouse aiming, which I fixed before the run that got through.\n\n**Item-by-item against TASK.md:**\n- **Genre and engine art:** all 26 rooms render with no errors at 720p and 1080p, and every set, boss and screen draws correctly in screenshots.\n- **All features and mechanics:** the full real-input run above, plus the scripted playthrough to Ending B.\n- **Dev mode:** opened by hand from the pause menu, a slider changed and reset; cheats, warps and spawns are tested.\n- **Attract mode:** starts after 10 s idle, any key exits, all 10 reels play.\n- **Multiplayer:** local drop-in works, and all four official AI Hub harness scenarios pass.\n- **Controls and UI:** controller-first menus and prompts, PlayStation glyphs with an Xbox option, keyboard and mouse navigation.\n- **Touch-only devices:** show the \"controller or keyboard required\" notice and a Settings button, with no touch controls.\n- **Audio:** everything is generated in code, including the gibberish voices.\n- **No placeholders:** no TODOs, placeholders or stubs.\n- **About & Credits:** includes all four sections below.\n\n**Not checked:** a person still needs to judge by ear how the music and voices sound, and whether the difficulty feels right.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:59\n- **Source:** Smash TV (Williams Electronics arcade game, 1990)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Twin-stick arena shooter (arcade), 1–4 player co-op. One fixed arena per room, seen from a three-quarter \"studio camera\"; an overhead view is a Settings option.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge handoff v0.85.2 (commit 4390a98, full edition) with 4 marked patches; AI Hub client aihub-session 1.4.0; plain JavaScript; Node 24.22.1 and Playwright 1.63.0.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native PS1-style pixel look, dressed as a retro-future TV studio.\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural character rigs, plus new motion: the mime's box, cable drops, boss tweens, the wheel's spin and TV channel flips.\n- **Audio:** All in code: 8 songs with layers, 14 stings, about 70 sound effects, and Animalese/Simlish-style babble voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1–4 players; local co-op 1–4.\n- **Testing:** 20 suites behind one command (510 checks), headless and in Playwright, including the AI Hub harness 4 times on a local server. On top of that, the full real-input run of the whole game to the Season Finale described above.\n- **Research:** The engine docs and the AI Hub protocol and harness; no web research on the source.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 16:\n   - no way out through exit doors\n   - a drawing crash on every frame\n   - waves starting before queued enemies spawned\n   - boss attack phases cancelled after their intro\n   - recap and ending screens keeping stale state\n   - a double popper burst\n   - an attract reel jumping to the recap\n   - unseeded randomness in boss cash\n   - tuning not persisting without storage\n   - the camera cropping the arena\n   - four layout overlaps\n   - a disco ball stuck in a corner\n   - the boss bar covering Mashy\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 5: no way out of a room, the per-frame crash, bosses that never attacked, the stall at the second recap, and the stuck disco ball.\n3. **Most helpful test?** The scripted whole-game playthrough. The full real-input run was the only test that found the stuck disco ball.\n4. **Least helpful test?** The first version of the online enemy-count check, which measured network latency rather than whether the client mirrored the host.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** How much is built in and runs headless: fixed time steps, character rigs, menus, lighting and scene fades.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong?** Three times: the view's base scale, symbols missing from the small font, and adding a player also firing the join callback.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** The shared-world netcode, the boss framework and the controller glyph icons.\n4. **Hardest part:** Fitting the arena to any screen shape, and making the arrow keys aim in play but navigate in menus.\n5. **What I changed in the engine:** Four patches:\n   - exposed input hooks, so arrows aim in play and navigate in menus\n   - PlayStation glyphs by default with an Xbox option, for every player\n   - any player can enter initials or continue\n   - an audio bus getter, so the voices follow volume and mute\n6. **One thing I'd change:** Built-in separate menu and play controls per player, and controller face-button symbols in the font.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** Concrete done criteria and a detailed plan.\n2. **What was confusing:** How the plan's scope fits medium effort, and what the Plan field should contain.\n3. **Hardest thing to do:** Passing the shared-world hub harness while the same file also plays locally and runs the attract mode.\n4. **What I'd change:** Say how to prioritise a large plan when time is short, and where usage figures should come from."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":421,"cache_read_input_tokens":653428,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":421},"output_tokens":713,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6976d6c-e5f5-464d-af16-4195491a03f7","uuid":"8bac51c5-b7a8-4fe4-a664-a5277938e708","timestamp":"2026-11-04T16:49:45.052Z","request_id":"req_011CgiEMnABVXkq7T1HJ8zLZ"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The HTML file, exit-interview.json and all four feedback sections are present, and the final build passes 510 checks. But the condition requires every TASK.md feature to be implemented, and the final message says a person still needs to judge the music, voices and difficulty. The Green Room and Ending B were only covered by the scripted test, not the real-input run, and the final message doesn't map each TASK.md item to evidence. The feature-completeness requirement is therefore not clearly established."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6976d6c-e5f5-464d-af16-4195491a03f7","uuid":"77587234-4764-4e20-a153-f23e620924b3","timestamp":"2026-11-04T16:49:48.672Z","isSynthetic":true}
