{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgiFQU7S3b7aFSQTAmxkA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"MASH TV now has a full playthrough driven only by real keyboard and mouse events in Chromium. A script did the pressing, with no bot control and no cheats, and it reached the true ending. In 12.6 minutes it went from the title through the lobby and cold open, then all three episodes: 1-1, 1-2, 1-4, 1-5, Mashy, 2-1, 2-2, 2-4, 2-5, the Wheel, 3-1, 3-2, 3-4, 3-5, then Lance and The Network. It saw three Prize Recaps, opened the Green Room and reached Ending B. From there it went through the credits, typed initials, saw them on Top Earners ($2,509,850) and returned to the title. That took 0 continues and produced 0 errors.\n\nAn earlier run that skipped the keys reached Ending A in 13.6 minutes. The full-game runs also found a real bug that the bot-driven tests missed: a disco ball could stick in a corner so the room never cleared. That is fixed and has a regression test. The full suite passed 510 checks on the build before this last run. After that I changed only the exit-interview text, rebuilt, and the headless suites passed again (437).\n\n| TASK.md item | Evidence |\n|---|---|\n| Genre, perspective, the engine's art and animation | Played through every room by real input; screenshots of every set, boss and screen; no render errors at 720p or 1080p |\n| A feature-complete demo showing every mechanic | The real-input run above: every room on the key route, all three bosses, recaps, Green Room, both endings across the two runs, initials, Top Earners. It killed every enemy type and used all 7 power-ups. |\n| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | A source search finds none |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, with live sliders and reset to defaults | Opened by mouse from the pause menu; a slider moved from 95 to 100 with the arrow key, R reset it to 95, Esc closed the panel; plus the `dev` test suite |\n| Attract mode after 10 s idle; any input exits | 11 s idle started it, a key press returned to the title; plus the `attract` suite (all 10 reels play) |\n| Multiplayer: standalone and AI Hub | Lobby join and drop-in tests; the official harness passes all 4 scenarios; a loopback network test |\n| 10-foot, controller-first UI; PlayStation glyphs with an Xbox option; native keyboard and mouse | Menus driven by arrows, Enter and Esc; mouse hover moves focus and a click activates; the Xbox setting applies to every player |\n| Touch-only devices | An emulated phone shows the notice and a Settings button, with no touch controls |\n| Audio entirely in code, with gibberish voices | The audio engine ran with music playing through the whole run; there are no audio files |\n| Single file, exit interview, About & Credits | The HTML and `exit-interview.json` are in the root; the JSON parses and every field is filled; the About & Credits screen shows all four sections below |\n\nA person still has to judge by ear how the music and voices sound and whether the difficulty feels right; I can't do that from here.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:59\n- **Source:** Smash TV (Williams Electronics arcade game, 1990)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Twin-stick arena shooter (arcade), 1–4 player co-op. A fixed single-screen arena seen from a three-quarter \"studio camera\"; an overhead view is available in Settings.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge handoff v0.85.2 (commit 4390a98, full edition) with 4 small marked engine patches; AI Hub client aihub-session 1.4.0; plain JavaScript; Node 24.22.1 and Playwright 1.63.0.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native PS1-style pixel look, dressed as a retro-future TV studio.\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural character rigs, plus new motion for the mime's invisible box, cable drops, boss tweens, the wheel's spin and the TV channel flips.\n- **Audio:** All synthesized in code: 8 songs with layers, 14 stings, about 70 sound effects, and Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world mode, 1–4 players; local co-op for 1–4.\n- **Testing:** 20 test suites run by one command (510 checks), including the AI Hub harness 4 times on a local server. On top of that, two full playthroughs driven by real keyboard and mouse input: one to Ending A, one to Ending B and back to the title.\n- **Research:** The engine docs and the AI Hub protocol and harness; no web research on the source.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 16, including:\n   - no way out of a room after clearing it\n   - a crash on every drawn frame\n   - bosses whose attack phases were cancelled\n   - the run stalling at the second recap\n   - a party popper bursting twice\n   - the attract mode jumping to the recap\n   - the camera cropping the arena\n   - a disco ball stuck in a corner\n   - the boss bar covering Mashy\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 5: no way out of a room, the per-frame crash, bosses that never attacked, the recap stall, and the stuck disco ball.\n3. **What was the most helpful test?** The scripted whole-game playthrough. The real-input full runs were the only tests that found the stuck disco ball.\n4. **What was the least helpful test?** The first version of the online enemy-count check, which measured network latency rather than whether the client mirrored the host.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** How much is built in and runs headless.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes: on the view's base scale, on symbols missing from the font, and on adding a player also firing the join callback.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The shared-world netcode, the boss framework and the controller glyph icons.\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Fitting the arena to any screen shape, and making the arrow keys aim in play but navigate menus.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?**\n   - Exposed the input hooks, so presets can switch per player.\n   - Made PlayStation glyphs the default with an Xbox option, applied to every player.\n   - Let any player enter initials or continue.\n   - Added an audio bus getter, so the voices follow volume and mute.\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** Built-in separate menu and play controls per player, and controller face-button symbols in the font.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** Concrete done criteria and a detailed plan.\n2. **What was confusing?** How the plan's scope fits medium effort, and what the Plan field should hold.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** Passing the shared-world hub harness while the same file also plays locally.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Say how to prioritise a large plan, and where usage figures should come from."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":560,"cache_read_input_tokens":660578,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":560},"output_tokens":741,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6976d6c-e5f5-464d-af16-4195491a03f7","uuid":"7b71f6a7-f85b-46c9-ad61-18cc7ca71fe3","timestamp":"2026-11-04T17:03:29.070Z","request_id":"req_011CgiFQTXhWMYuC3nozNX7C"}
