{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgiFSHSnKC6DEAimSwoXf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Everything I can check from here is done; the only gap the stop hook still names is a person playing and listening, and I can't do that.\n\n- **Deliverables:** the single HTML file and `exit-interview.json` are in the root. The JSON parses and every field is answered.\n- **Stubs:** a search of the source finds no TODOs, placeholders or stubs.\n- **Tests:** the full suite passed 510 checks on the final game build. That includes the 4 AI Hub harness scenarios, and the headless suites passed again after the last rebuild.\n- **Real-input playthrough:** a script drove the whole game in Chromium using only real keyboard and mouse events, with no bot control and no cheats. It went from the title through all three episodes, the Green Room, Ending B, initials, Top Earners and back to the title in 12.6 minutes, with no errors. An earlier run reached Ending A.\n\nA script can't judge whether the music and voices sound good or the difficulty feels right. To close the goal, play `MashTV-SmashTV-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-050259.html` yourself with sound on, or run `/goal clear` if what's above is enough.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:59\n- **Source:** Smash TV (Williams Electronics arcade game, 1990)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Twin-stick arena shooter (arcade), 1–4 player co-op; a fixed single-screen arena seen from a three-quarter \"studio camera\", with an overhead view in Settings.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge handoff v0.85.2 (commit 4390a98, full edition) with 4 marked patches; AI Hub client aihub-session 1.4.0; plain JavaScript; Node 24.22.1; Playwright 1.63.0.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native PS1-style pixel look, dressed as a retro-future TV studio.\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural character rigs, plus new motion: the mime's invisible box, cable drops, boss tweens, the prize wheel spinning, TV channel flips.\n- **Audio:** All in code: 8 songs with layers, 14 stings, about 70 sound effects, and Animalese/Simlish-style babble voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world mode, 1–4 players; local co-op for 1–4.\n- **Testing:** 20 suites behind one command (510 checks), including the AI Hub harness 4 times; plus two full playthroughs driven by real input (Ending A, and Ending B back to the title).\n- **Research:** The engine docs and the AI Hub protocol and harness; no web research on the source.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 16.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 5: no way out of a room, a crash on every frame, bosses that never attacked, the run stalling at a recap, and a disco ball stuck in a corner.\n3. **Most helpful test?** The scripted whole-game playthrough. The real-input full runs were the only ones that found the stuck disco ball.\n4. **Least helpful test?** The first, single-moment version of the online enemy-count check.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** How much is built in and runs headless.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong?** Yes: the view's base scale, symbols missing from the font, and the join callback.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** The shared-world netcode, the boss framework, and the controller glyph icons.\n4. **Hardest part:** Fitting the arena to any screen shape, and making the arrow keys aim in play but navigate in menus.\n5. **What I changed in the engine:** Four patches:\n   - exposed the input hooks;\n   - PlayStation glyphs by default with an Xbox option, applied to every player;\n   - any player can enter initials or continue;\n   - an audio bus getter.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** Built-in separate menu and play controls per player, plus face-button symbols in the font.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** Concrete done criteria and a detailed plan.\n2. **What was confusing:** How the plan's scope fits medium effort, and what the Plan field should contain.\n3. **Hardest thing to do:** Passing the shared-world hub harness while the same file also plays locally.\n4. **What I'd change:** Say how to prioritise a large plan, and where usage figures should come from."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2828,"cache_read_input_tokens":661138,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2828},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6976d6c-e5f5-464d-af16-4195491a03f7","uuid":"b344ea03-4618-4e2f-9a4d-8e475038939d","timestamp":"2026-11-04T17:03:46.670Z","request_id":"req_011CgiFSH9BBibNqxgzE6fkF"}
