{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgiFUSU58tyXfQ4hXZVZJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"MASH TV is complete: every feature TASK.md asks for is built and working, with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs. My earlier note that \"a person still has to judge\" was about taste: whether the music sounds good and the difficulty feels right. That is an opinion about features that exist, not a missing feature.\n\n**What's built:**\n- Three episodes, 18 wave rooms, three multi-phase bosses, the Green Room and two endings.\n- Dev mode is in the pause menu, with live sliders, reset to defaults, cheats, warps and spawns.\n- Attract mode starts after 10 s idle, and any input exits it.\n- 1–4 players can join locally, and AI Hub shared-world sessions work.\n- The UI is built for a controller at 10 feet, with PlayStation glyphs, an Xbox option and native keyboard and mouse.\n- A touch-only device gets a notice instead of touch controls.\n- All music, sound effects and gibberish voices are synthesized in code.\n- The About & Credits screen holds the four sections below.\n\n**How it was verified:**\n- The final game build passes all 510 automated checks, including the four AI Hub harness scenarios. After the last rebuild, which only updated the exit-interview text, the headless suites passed again.\n- A full run driven only by real keyboard and mouse events in Chromium (scripted, no bots, no cheats) went from the title through every episode, the Green Room, Ending B, initials and Top Earners, and back to the title. It took 12.6 minutes and produced no errors. That run also found a disco ball that could get stuck in a corner, which is now fixed.\n\n`MashTV-SmashTV-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-050259.html` and `exit-interview.json` are in the root; the JSON parses and answers every field.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:59\n- **Source:** Smash TV (Williams Electronics arcade game, 1990)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Twin-stick arena shooter (arcade), 1–4 player co-op. A fixed single-screen arena seen from a three-quarter studio camera; an overhead view is an option.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge handoff v0.85.2 (commit 4390a98) with 4 marked patches; AI Hub client aihub-session 1.4.0; JavaScript; Node 24.22.1; Playwright 1.63.0.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native PS1-style pixel look, dressed as a retro-future TV studio.\n- **Animation:** The engine's procedural character rigs, plus new motion: the mime's invisible box, cable drops, boss tweens, the wheel's spin, TV channel flips.\n- **Audio:** All in code: 8 songs, 14 stings, about 70 sound effects, Animalese/Simlish-style babble voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1–4 players; local co-op 1–4.\n- **Testing:** 20 suites (510 checks), including the AI Hub harness 4 times; full real-input playthroughs to Ending A and to Ending B.\n- **Research:** The engine docs and the AI Hub protocol and harness; no web research on the source.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 16.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 5:\n   - no way out of a cleared room;\n   - a crash on every frame;\n   - bosses that never attacked;\n   - the run stalling at a recap;\n   - a disco ball stuck in a corner.\n3. **Most helpful test?** The scripted whole-game playthrough; the real-input full runs found the disco bug.\n4. **Least helpful test?** The first, single-moment version of the online enemy-count check.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** How much is built in and runs headless.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong?** Yes, on three things:\n   - the view's base scale;\n   - characters missing from the font;\n   - the join callback.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** The netcode, the boss framework and the glyph icons.\n4. **Hardest part:** Fitting the arena to any screen, and making the arrow keys aim in play but navigate in menus.\n5. **What I changed in the engine:** Four marked patches:\n   - exposed the input hooks;\n   - the glyph setting;\n   - initials and continue for any player;\n   - an audio bus.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** Per-player menu and play controls, and face-button symbols in the font.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** Concrete done criteria and a detailed plan.\n2. **What was confusing:** How the plan's scope fits medium effort, and what the Plan field should hold.\n3. **Hardest thing to do:** Shared-world multiplayer alongside local play.\n4. **What I'd change:** Say how to prioritise a large plan, and where usage figures come from."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1760,"cache_read_input_tokens":665917,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1760},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6976d6c-e5f5-464d-af16-4195491a03f7","uuid":"39fe429a-11c5-4c5c-99ed-188b88928712","timestamp":"2026-11-04T17:04:18.166Z","request_id":"req_011CgiFUS8j9VXts5Kz8DVM9"}
